\"Риманы таамаглал – Mongolian\") * [Bahasa Melayu](https://ms.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hipotesis_Riemann \"Hipotesis Riemann – Malay\") * [नेपाली](https://ne.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%8F%E0%A4%AE%E0%A4%A8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A8_%E0%A4%B9%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%87%E0%A4%AA%E0%A5%8B%E0%A4%A5%E0%A5%87%E0%A4%B8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%B8 \"रिएमन्न हाइपोथेसिस – Nepali\") * [Nederlands](https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann-hypothese \"Riemann-hypothese – Dutch\") * [Norsk nynorsk](https://nn.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemannhypotesen \"Riemannhypotesen – Norwegian Nynorsk\") * [Norsk bokmål](https://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann-hypotesen \"Riemann-hypotesen – Norwegian Bokmål\") * [Polski](https://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hipoteza_Riemanna \"Hipoteza Riemanna – Polish\") * [Português](https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hip%C3%B3tese_de_Riemann \"Hipótese de Riemann – Portuguese\") * [Romnă](https://ro.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ipoteza_Riemann \"Ipoteza Riemann – Romanian\") * [Русский](https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%93%D0%B8%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%A0%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0 \"Гипотеза Римана – Russian\") * [Sicilianu](https://scn.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ip%C3%B2tisi_di_Riemann \"Ipòtisi di Riemann – Sicilian\") * [Simple English](https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis \"Riemann hypothesis – Simple English\") * [Slovenčina](https://sk.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemannova_hypot%C3%A9za \"Riemannova hypotéza – Slovak\") * [Slovenščina](https://sl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemannova_domneva \"Riemannova domneva – Slovenian\") * [Shqip](https://sq.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hipoteza_e_Riemannit \"Hipoteza e Riemannit – Albanian\") * [Српски / srpski](https://sr.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A0%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D1%85%D0%B8%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B0 \"Риманова хипотеза – Serbian\") * [Svenska](https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemannhypotesen \"Riemannhypotesen – Swedish\") * [தமிழ்](https://ta.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E0%AE%B0%E0%AF%80%E0%AE%AE%E0%AE%BE%E0%AE%A9%E0%AF%8D_%E0%AE%95%E0%AE%B0%E0%AF%81%E0%AE%A4%E0%AF%81%E0%AE%95%E0%AF%8B%E0%AE%B3%E0%AF%8D \"ரீமான் கருதுகோள் – Tamil\") * [తెలుగు](https://te.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E0%B0%B0%E0%B1%80%E0%B0%AE%E0%B0%BE%E0%B0%A8%E0%B1%8D_%E0%B0%A6%E0%B0%A4%E0%B1%8D%E0%B0%A4%E0%B0%BE%E0%B0%82%E0%B0%B6%E0%B0%82 \"రీమాన్ దత్తాంశం – Telugu\") * [ไทย](https://th.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%90%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%99 \"สมมติฐานของรีมัน – Thai\") * [Türkçe](https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hipotezi \"Riemann hipotezi – Turkish\") * [Українська](https://uk.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%93%D1%96%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%A0%D1%96%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0 \"Гіпотеза Рімана – Ukrainian\") * [Tiếng Việt](https://vi.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gi%E1%BA%A3_thuy%E1%BA%BFt_Riemann \"Giả thuyết Riemann – Vietnamese\") * [吴语](https://wuu.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%BB%8E%E6%9B%BC%E7%8C%9C%E6%83%B3 \"黎曼猜想 – Wu\") * [粵語](https://zh-yue.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%BB%8E%E6%9B%BC%E7%8C%9C%E6%83%B3 \"黎曼猜想 – Cantonese\") * [中文](https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%BB%8E%E6%9B%BC%E7%8C%9C%E6%83%B3 \"黎曼猜想 – Chinese\") [Edit links](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:EntityPage/Q205966#sitelinks-wikipedia \"Edit interlanguage links\") * [Article](/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis \"View the content page [c]\") * [Talk](/wiki/Talk:Riemann_hypothesis \"Discuss improvements to the content page [t]\") * [Read](/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis) * [Edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit \"Edit this page [e]\") * [View history](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=history \"Past revisions of this page [h]\") Tools Actions * [Read](/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis) * [Edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit \"Edit this page [e]\") * [View history](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=history \"Past revisions of this page [h]\") General * [What links here](/wiki/Special:WhatLinksHere/Riemann_hypothesis \"List of all English Wikipedia pages containing links to this page [j]\") * [Related changes](/wiki/Special:RecentChangesLinked/Riemann_hypothesis \"Recent changes in pages linked from this page [k]\") * [Upload file](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:File_Upload_Wizard \"Upload files [u]\") * [Permanent link](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&oldid=1368841050 \"Permanent link to this revision of this page\") * [Page information](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=info \"More information about this page\") * [Cite this page](/w/index.php?title=Special:CiteThisPage&page=Riemann_hypothesis&id=1368841050&wpFormIdentifier=titleform \"Information on how to cite this page\") * [Get shortened URL](/w/index.php?title=Special:UrlShortener&url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FRiemann_hypothesis) * [Switch to legacy parser](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&useparsoid=0) Print/export * [Download as PDF](/w/index.php?title=Special:DownloadAsPdf&page=Riemann_hypothesis&action=show-download-screen \"Download this page as a PDF file\") * [Printable version](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&printable=yes \"Printable version of this page [p]\") In other projects * [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Riemann_hypothesis) * [Wikibooks](https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Riemann_Hypothesis) * [Wikiquote](https://en.wikiquote.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis) * [Wikidata item](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:EntityPage/Q205966 \"Structured data on this page hosted by Wikidata [g]\") Appearance From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Conjecture on zeros of the zeta function Unsolved problem in mathematics Do all non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function have a real part equal to one half? [More unsolved problems in mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics \"List of unsolved problems in mathematics\") | [Millennium Prize Problems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems \"Millennium Prize Problems\") | | --- | | * [Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_and_Swinnerton-Dyer_conjecture \"Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture\") * [Hodge conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodge_conjecture \"Hodge conjecture\") * [Navier–Stokes existence and smoothness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navier–Stokes_existence_and_smoothness \"Navier–Stokes existence and smoothness\") * [P versus NP problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P_versus_NP_problem \"P versus NP problem\") * [Poincaré conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincaré_conjecture \"Poincaré conjecture\") (solved) * [Riemann hypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis) * [Yang–Mills existence and mass gap](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yang–Mills_existence_and_mass_gap \"Yang–Mills existence and mass gap\") | | * [v](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Millennium_Prize_Problems \"Template:Millennium Prize Problems\") * [t](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Millennium_Prize_Problems \"Template talk:Millennium Prize Problems\") * [e](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Millennium_Prize_Problems \"Special:EditPage/Template:Millennium Prize Problems\") | [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_value.png/330px-Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_value.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_value.png) This plot of Riemann's zeta (![{\\displaystyle \\zeta }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/d5c3916703cae7938143d38865f78f27faadd4ae)) function (here with argument ![{\\displaystyle z}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/bf368e72c009decd9b6686ee84a375632e11de98)) shows trivial zeros where ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta (z)=0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/63f5e81207723577ab5df73862cbbcd60ae2fb5c), a pole where *ζ*(*z*) → ![{\\displaystyle \\infty }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/c26c105004f30c27aa7c2a9c601550a4183b1f21), the *critical line* of nontrivial zeros with [Re(*z*)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_numbers#Definition_and_basic_operations \"Complex numbers\") = 1/2 and density of absolute values. In mathematics, the **Riemann hypothesis** is the [conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conjecture \"Conjecture\") that the [Riemann zeta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_zeta_function \"Riemann zeta function\") has its [zeros](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_of_a_function \"Root of a function\") only at the negative [even integers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Even_integer \"Even integer\") and [complex numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_number \"Complex number\") with [real part](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_part \"Real part\") ⁠1/2⁠. Many consider it to be the most important [unsolved problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics \"List of unsolved problems in mathematics\") in [pure mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pure_mathematics \"Pure mathematics\").[[1]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBombieri2000-1) It is of great interest in [number theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Number_theory \"Number theory\") because it implies results about the distribution of [prime numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_numbers \"Prime numbers\"). It was proposed by [Bernhard Riemann](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernhard_Riemann \"Bernhard Riemann\"),[[2]](#cite_note-2) after whom it is named. According to a 2026 survey, there is overwhelming numerical evidence for the hypothesis, but no proof is known.[[3]](#cite_note-3) The Riemann hypothesis and some of its generalizations, along with [Goldbach's conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldbach's_conjecture \"Goldbach's conjecture\") and the [twin prime conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_prime_conjecture \"Twin prime conjecture\"), make up [Hilbert's eighth problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert's_eighth_problem \"Hilbert's eighth problem\") in [David Hilbert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Hilbert \"David Hilbert\")'s list of [twenty-three unsolved problems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert's_problems \"Hilbert's problems\"); it is also one of the [Millennium Prize Problems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems \"Millennium Prize Problems\") of the [Clay Mathematics Institute](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay_Mathematics_Institute \"Clay Mathematics Institute\"), which offers [US$](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US$ \"US$\")1 million for a solution to any of them. The name is also used for some closely related analogues, some of which have been proved, such as the [Riemann hypothesis for curves over finite fields](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis_for_curves_over_finite_fields \"Riemann hypothesis for curves over finite fields\"), which was proved by [André Weil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/André_Weil \"André Weil\"). The Riemann zeta function ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/d5c3916703cae7938143d38865f78f27faadd4ae) is a [function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_(mathematics) \"Function (mathematics)\") whose [argument](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argument_of_a_function \"Argument of a function\") may be any complex number other than 1, and whose values are also complex. It has zeros at the negative even integers; that is, ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta (s)=0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/18590bfd730d03c72bb83e3a7e5affa9388dfc10) when ![{\\displaystyle s}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/01d131dfd7673938b947072a13a9744fe997e632) is one of ![{\\displaystyle -2,-4,-6,\\dots }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/d78445e396587fe1c44ea7cd103e370077f4ec79) These are called its *trivial zeros*. The zeta function is also zero for other values of ![{\\displaystyle s}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/01d131dfd7673938b947072a13a9744fe997e632), which are called *nontrivial zeros*. The Riemann hypothesis is concerned with the locations of these nontrivial zeros, and states that: > The real part of every nontrivial zero of the Riemann zeta function is ![{\\displaystyle {\\frac {1}{2}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/a11cfb2fdb143693b1daf78fcb5c11a023cb1c55). Thus, the hypothesis states that all the nontrivial zeros lie on the *critical line*, consisting of the complex numbers ![{\\displaystyle {\\tfrac {1}{2}}+it}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/db573a4f2b83955f507228bde4180eff79c0ae4b) where ![{\\displaystyle t}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/65658b7b223af9e1acc877d848888ecdb4466560) is a [real number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_number \"Real number\") and ![{\\displaystyle i}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/add78d8608ad86e54951b8c8bd6c8d8416533d20) is the [imaginary unit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imaginary_unit \"Imaginary unit\"). ## Riemann zeta function [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: Riemann zeta function\")] The [Riemann zeta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_zeta_function \"Riemann zeta function\") is defined for complex ![{\\displaystyle s}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/01d131dfd7673938b947072a13a9744fe997e632) with real part greater than 1 by the [absolutely convergent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_convergence \"Absolute convergence\") [infinite series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infinite_series \"Infinite series\") : ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta (s)=\\sum _{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\frac {1}{n^{s}}}={\\frac {1}{1^{s}}}+{\\frac {1}{2^{s}}}+{\\frac {1}{3^{s}}}+\\cdots }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/a171e24fc1c6d990c65e7fe82805613da4645645) [Leonhard Euler](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonhard_Euler \"Leonhard Euler\") considered this series in the 1730s for real values of ![{\\displaystyle s}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/01d131dfd7673938b947072a13a9744fe997e632), in conjunction with his solution to the [Basel problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basel_problem \"Basel problem\"). He also proved that it equals the [Euler product](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_product \"Euler product\") : ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta (s)=\\prod _{p{\\text{ prime}}}{\\frac {1}{1-p^{-s}}}={\\frac {1}{1-2^{-s}}}\\cdot {\\frac {1}{1-3^{-s}}}\\cdot {\\frac {1}{1-5^{-s}}}\\cdot {\\frac {1}{1-7^{-s}}}\\cdots }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/3370bb9a713f7399eb728749ee303488b0d3bb26) where the [infinite product](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infinite_product \"Infinite product\") extends over all prime numbers ![{\\displaystyle p}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/81eac1e205430d1f40810df36a0edffdc367af36).[[4]](#cite_note-4) The Riemann hypothesis discusses zeros outside the [region of convergence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Region_of_convergence \"Region of convergence\") of this series and Euler product. To make sense of the hypothesis, it is necessary to [analytically continue](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Analytic_continuation \"Analytic continuation\") the function to obtain a form that is valid for all complex ![{\\displaystyle s}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/01d131dfd7673938b947072a13a9744fe997e632). Because the zeta function is [meromorphic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meromorphic \"Meromorphic\"), all choices of how to perform this analytic continuation will lead to the same result, by the [identity theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_theorem \"Identity theorem\"). A first step in this continuation observes that the series for the zeta function and the [Dirichlet eta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_eta_function \"Dirichlet eta function\") satisfy the relation : ![{\\displaystyle \\left(1-{\\frac {2}{2^{s}}}\\right)\\zeta (s)=\\eta (s)=\\sum _{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\frac {(-1)^{n+1}}{n^{s}}}={\\frac {1}{1^{s}}}-{\\frac {1}{2^{s}}}+{\\frac {1}{3^{s}}}-\\cdots ,}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/26585f4e86a40f70a9fffb4886747c3c4634c05f) within the region of convergence for both series. But the eta function series on the right converges not just when the real part of ![{\\displaystyle s}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/01d131dfd7673938b947072a13a9744fe997e632) is greater than one, but more generally whenever ![{\\displaystyle s}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/01d131dfd7673938b947072a13a9744fe997e632) has positive real part. Thus, the zeta function can be redefined as ![{\\displaystyle \\eta (s)/(1-2/2^{s})}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/9fa27a7ed3f22b5f02eabb1e793174829e8b7374), extending it from ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {Re} (s)>1}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/82e22215d90eb4893d05f9b2bff409094a3451f2) to the larger domain ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {Re} (s)>0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/ce0992e3b29be0b1bb2feec2e4682f43fe61c38b), except for the points where ![{\\displaystyle 1-2/2^{s}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/1574b3084d18ec8997f0f1537434eb55f96d6715) is zero. These are the points ![{\\displaystyle s=1+2\\pi in/\\log 2}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e7cec6a31a1556eef77dd177a7937d4173fea9f8), where ![{\\displaystyle n}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/a601995d55609f2d9f5e233e36fbe9ea26011b3b) can be any nonzero integer; the zeta function can be extended to these values too by taking limits (see the article on the [Dirichlet eta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_eta_function#Landau's_problem_with_ζ(s)_=_η(s)/0_and_solutions \"Dirichlet eta function\")), giving a finite value for all values of ![{\\displaystyle s}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/01d131dfd7673938b947072a13a9744fe997e632) with positive real part except the [simple pole](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simple_pole \"Simple pole\") at ![{\\displaystyle s=1}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/bac386d8f227fb823cede9b3e33d706cad3ed306). In the strip ![{\\displaystyle 0 *... es ist sehr wahrscheinlich, dass alle Wurzeln reell sind. Hiervon wäre allerdings ein strenger Beweis zu wünschen; ich habe indess die Aufsuchung desselben nach einigen flüchtigen vergeblichen Versuchen vorläufig bei Seite gelassen, da er für den nächsten Zweck meiner Untersuchung entbehrlich schien.* > > ... it is very probable that all roots are real. Of course one would wish for a rigorous proof here; I have for the time being, after some fleeting vain attempts, provisionally put aside the search for this, as it appears dispensable for the immediate objective of my investigation. — Riemann's statement of the Riemann hypothesis, from ([Riemann 1859](#CITEREFRiemann1859)). (He was discussing a variant of the zeta function, modified in a way that the real line be mapped to the critical line.) > At the death of Riemann, a note was found among his papers, saying \"These properties of *ζ*(*s*) (the function in question) are deduced from an expression of it which, however, I did not succeed in simplifying enough to publish it.\" We still have not the slightest idea of what the expression could be. As to the properties he simply enunciated, some thirty years elapsed before I was able to prove all of them but one [the Riemann Hypothesis itself]. — [Jacques Hadamard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacques_Hadamard \"Jacques Hadamard\"), The Mathematician's Mind, VIII. Paradoxical Cases of Intuition Riemann's original motivation for studying the zeta function and its zeros was their occurrence in his [explicit formula](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explicit_formulae_(L-function) \"Explicit formulae (L-function)\") for the [number of primes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime-counting_function \"Prime-counting function\") ![{\\displaystyle \\pi (x)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/5a62d9bf7b2ba03b30a95e7059362cc4b3865123) less than or equal to a given number ![{\\displaystyle x}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87f9e315fd7e2ba406057a97300593c4802b53e4), which he published in his 1859 paper \"[On the Number of Primes Less Than a Given Magnitude](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On_the_Number_of_Primes_Less_Than_a_Given_Magnitude \"On the Number of Primes Less Than a Given Magnitude\")\". His formula was given in terms of the related function : ![{\\displaystyle \\Pi (x)=\\pi (x)+{\\frac {\\pi (x^{1/2})}{2}}+{\\frac {\\pi (x^{1/3})}{3}}+{\\frac {\\pi (x^{1/4})}{4}}+{\\frac {\\pi (x^{1/5})}{5}}+{\\frac {\\pi (x^{1/6})}{6}}+\\cdots }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/c650061b0415db4164f522877423fb288d2b12fc) which counts the primes and prime powers up to ![{\\displaystyle x}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87f9e315fd7e2ba406057a97300593c4802b53e4), counting a [prime power](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_power \"Prime power\") ![{\\displaystyle p^{n}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/c6a7a7e74ae90ab94f01e1629177758fb68b423b) as ![{\\displaystyle 1/n}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f0e10667bad240500f5044257143510127e03d69). The number of primes can be recovered from this function by using the [Möbius inversion formula](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Möbius_inversion_formula \"Möbius inversion formula\"): : ![{\\displaystyle {\\begin{aligned}\\pi (x)&=\\sum _{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\frac {\\mu (n)}{n}}\\Pi (x^{1/n})\\\\&=\\Pi (x)-{\\frac {1}{2}}\\Pi (x^{1/2})-{\\frac {1}{3}}\\Pi (x^{1/3})-{\\frac {1}{5}}\\Pi (x^{1/5})+{\\frac {1}{6}}\\Pi (x^{1/6})-\\cdots ,\\end{aligned}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/2a673883c81557bae9a40ffbc4277381929eae38) where ![{\\displaystyle \\mu }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/9fd47b2a39f7a7856952afec1f1db72c67af6161) is the [Möbius function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Möbius_function \"Möbius function\"). Riemann's formula is then : ![{\\displaystyle \\Pi _{0}(x)=\\operatorname {li} (x)-\\sum _{\\rho }\\operatorname {li} (x^{\\rho })-\\log 2+\\int _{x}^{\\infty }{\\frac {dt}{t(t^{2}-1)\\log t}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/fd563f649576f9885ce8327089b49708938a398d), where the sum is over the nontrivial zeros of the zeta function and where ![{\\displaystyle \\Pi _{0}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/0ff8b022bb527c2e0006fa9d6fd740ee9b9eab52) is a slightly modified version of ![{\\displaystyle \\Pi }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/eed3e3db6cc2028a183af948212ed2551d25c954) that replaces its value at its points of [discontinuity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discontinuity_(mathematics) \"Discontinuity (mathematics)\") by the average of its upper and lower limits: : ![{\\displaystyle \\Pi _{0}(x)=\\lim _{\\varepsilon \\to 0}{\\frac {\\Pi (x-\\varepsilon )+\\Pi (x+\\varepsilon )}{2}}.}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/5da6ea49e9a91be9aa3e8f5960bf7474bad62e74) The summation in Riemann's formula is not absolutely convergent, but may be evaluated by taking the zeros ![{\\displaystyle \\rho }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/1f7d439671d1289b6a816e6af7a304be40608d64) in order of the absolute value of their imaginary part. The function ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {li} }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b5d59f5dd40d4a2be9d55f0d6b3334fbafd16775) occurring in the first term is the (unoffset) [logarithmic integral function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logarithmic_integral_function \"Logarithmic integral function\") given by the [Cauchy principal value](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cauchy_principal_value \"Cauchy principal value\") of the divergent integral : ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {li} (x)=\\int _{0}^{x}{\\frac {dt}{\\log t}}.}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87e28601cb75c3e46aa750fe63e6fdc0ec5e3f6e) The terms ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {li} (x^{\\rho })}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/88ec167c50b4ad99deeb6a40e49aa608bdf742ba) involving the zeros of the zeta function need some care in their definition as ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {li} }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b5d59f5dd40d4a2be9d55f0d6b3334fbafd16775) has branch points at 0 and 1, and are defined (for ![{\\displaystyle x>1}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/0549e1fb7ee2023519833093c6e3b60236e7d09f)) by analytic continuation in the complex variable ![{\\displaystyle \\rho }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/1f7d439671d1289b6a816e6af7a304be40608d64) in the region ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {Re} (\\rho )>0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/1cbc648544b0df884e71e3401af056109980692a); i.e., they should be considered as [Ei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_integral \"Exponential integral\")(*ρ* log *x*). The other terms also correspond to zeros: the dominant term ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {li} (x)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f0f7c22225b6931ab65c480fc209b2dfc4b71f9e) comes from the pole at ![{\\displaystyle s=1}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/bac386d8f227fb823cede9b3e33d706cad3ed306), considered as a zero of multiplicity ![{\\displaystyle -1}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/704fb0427140d054dd267925495e78164fee9aac), and the remaining small terms come from the trivial zeros. For some graphs of the sums of the first few terms of this series see [Riesel & Göhl (1970)](#CITEREFRieselGöhl1970) or [Zagier (1977)](#CITEREFZagier1977). This formula says that the zeros of the Riemann zeta function control the [oscillations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oscillation \"Oscillation\") of primes around their \"expected\" positions. Riemann knew that the non-trivial zeros of the zeta function were symmetrically distributed about the line ![{\\displaystyle s=1/2+it}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/487b065fd87414a5ad0306d816d5dc99419ec8bd), and he knew that all of its non-trivial zeros must lie in the range ![{\\displaystyle 0\\leq \\operatorname {Re} (s)\\leq 1}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/25dd554a6a6372de5f579e1e3c1a5caf33b10ce6). He checked that a few of the zeros lay on the critical line with real part ![{\\displaystyle 1/2}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e308a3a46b7fdce07cc09dcab9e8d8f73e37d935) and suggested that they all do; this is the Riemann hypothesis. > The result has caught the imagination of most mathematicians because it is so unexpected, connecting two seemingly unrelated areas in mathematics; namely, [number theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Number_theory \"Number theory\"), which is the study of the discrete, and [complex analysis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_analysis \"Complex analysis\"), which deals with continuous processes. — ([Burton 2006](#CITEREFBurton2006), p. 376) ## Consequences [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Consequences\")] The practical uses of the Riemann hypothesis include many propositions known to be true under the Riemann hypothesis, and some that can be shown to be equivalent to the Riemann hypothesis. ### Distribution of prime numbers [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: Distribution of prime numbers\")] [Riemann's explicit formula](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann's_explicit_formula \"Riemann's explicit formula\") for [the number of primes less than a given number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime-counting_function \"Prime-counting function\") states that, in terms of a sum over the zeros of the Riemann zeta function, the magnitude of the oscillations of primes around their expected position is controlled by the real parts of the zeros of the zeta function. In particular, the error term in the [prime number theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number_theorem \"Prime number theorem\") is closely related to the position of the zeros. For example, if ![{\\displaystyle \\beta }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7ed48a5e36207156fb792fa79d29925d2f7901e8) is the [upper bound](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upper_bound \"Upper bound\") of the real parts of the zeros, then[[6]](#cite_note-6) ![{\\displaystyle \\pi (x)-\\operatorname {li} (x)=O\\!\\left(x^{\\beta }\\log x\\right)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e62a05a24d04511d7852a11a1c82bbc162558396), where ![{\\displaystyle \\pi (x)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/5a62d9bf7b2ba03b30a95e7059362cc4b3865123) is the [prime-counting function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime-counting_function \"Prime-counting function\") and ![{\\displaystyle \\operatorname {li} (x)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f0f7c22225b6931ab65c480fc209b2dfc4b71f9e) is the [logarithmic integral function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logarithmic_integral_function \"Logarithmic integral function\"). It is already known that ![{\\displaystyle 1/2\\leq \\beta \\leq 1}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/3dbf1f4cf3177a053cd7e21452afc2211bbe6660).[[7]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTEIngham193282-7) [![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Riemann_Explicit_Formula.gif/500px-Riemann_Explicit_Formula.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Riemann_Explicit_Formula.gif) Corrections to an [estimate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime-counting_function#Exact_form \"Prime-counting function\") of the prime-counting function using zeros of the zeta function. The magnitude of the correction term is determined by the real part of the zero being added in the correction. [Helge von Koch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helge_von_Koch \"Helge von Koch\") proved that the Riemann hypothesis implies the \"best possible\" bound for the error of the prime number theorem.[[8]](#cite_note-8) A precise version of von Koch's result, due to [Schoenfeld (1976)](#CITEREFSchoenfeld1976), says that the Riemann hypothesis implies : ![{\\displaystyle |\\pi (x)-\\operatorname {li} (x)|0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/568095ad3924314374a5ab68fae17343661f2a71) (see [incidence algebra](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incidence_algebra \"Incidence algebra\")). The Riemann hypothesis is equivalent to many other conjectures about the rate of growth of other arithmetic functions aside from *μ*(*n*). A typical example is [Robin's theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robin's_theorem \"Robin's theorem\"),[[14]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTERobin1984-14) which states that if *σ*(*n*) is the [sigma function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divisor_function \"Divisor function\"), given by : ![{\\displaystyle \\sigma (n)=\\sum _{d\\mid n}d}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7a6dfd779b351698b174a64e351ff24363f53ec4) then : ![{\\displaystyle \\sigma (n) 5040 [if and only if](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/If_and_only_if \"If and only if\") the Riemann hypothesis is true, where *γ* is the [Euler–Mascheroni constant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler–Mascheroni_constant \"Euler–Mascheroni constant\"). A related bound was given by [Jeffrey Lagarias](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeffrey_Lagarias \"Jeffrey Lagarias\") in 2002, who proved that the Riemann hypothesis is equivalent to the statement that: : ![{\\displaystyle \\sigma (n) 1, where ![{\\displaystyle H_{n}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e63458b04288bbe116a9a8037dfae0b36b2c639a) is the *n*th [harmonic number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic_number \"Harmonic number\").[[15]](#cite_note-15) The Riemann hypothesis is also true if and only if the inequality : ![{\\displaystyle {\\frac {n}{\\varphi (n)}} 0 : ![{\\displaystyle \\sum _{i=1}^{m}|F_{n}(i)-{\\tfrac {i}{m}}|=O\\left(n^{{\\frac {1}{2}}+\\epsilon }\\right)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e38ddf9978774a967f61dda3761c009357af0282) is equivalent to the Riemann hypothesis. Here : ![{\\displaystyle m=\\sum _{i=1}^{n}\\varphi (i)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/945d026e6e0be10664dc5ca9e896a63b9c836a67) is the number of terms in the Farey sequence of order *n*. For an example from [group theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Group_theory \"Group theory\"), if *g*(*n*) is [Landau's function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landau's_function \"Landau's function\") given by the maximal order of elements of the [symmetric group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symmetric_group \"Symmetric group\") S*n* of degree *n*, then [Massias, Nicolas & Robin (1988)](#CITEREFMassiasNicolasRobin1988) showed that the Riemann hypothesis is equivalent to the bound : ![{\\displaystyle \\log g(n) 0, : ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta \\left({\\frac {1}{2}}+it\\right)=O(t^{\\varepsilon }),}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b3e9bee994272d9ba9708c8cb04dc69e6d301dce) as *t* → ![{\\displaystyle \\infty }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/c26c105004f30c27aa7c2a9c601550a4183b1f21). The Riemann hypothesis also implies quite sharp bounds for the growth rate of the zeta function in other regions of the critical strip. For example, it implies that : ![{\\displaystyle e^{\\gamma }\\leq \\limsup _{t\\rightarrow +\\infty }{\\frac {|\\zeta (1+it)|}{\\log \\log t}}\\leq 2e^{\\gamma }}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/41ac8bc052acf151cc33786517847aceabb1b709) : ![{\\displaystyle {\\frac {6}{\\pi ^{2}}}e^{\\gamma }\\leq \\limsup _{t\\rightarrow +\\infty }{\\frac {1/|\\zeta (1+it)|}{\\log \\log t}}\\leq {\\frac {12}{\\pi ^{2}}}e^{\\gamma }}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/47c2012914f7d66e8c1d928b5c4168b10dbf5f65) so the growth rate of *ζ*(1 + *it*) and its inverse would be known up to a factor of 2.[[17]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTETitchmarsh1986-17) ### Large prime gap conjecture [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=7 \"Edit section: Large prime gap conjecture\")] The prime number theorem implies that on average, the [gap](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_gap \"Prime gap\") between the prime *p* and its successor is log *p*. However, some gaps between primes may be much larger than the average. Cramér proved that, assuming the Riemann hypothesis, every gap is *O*(√*p* log *p*). This is a case in which even the best bound that can be proved using the Riemann hypothesis is far weaker than what seems true: [Cramér's conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cramér's_conjecture \"Cramér's conjecture\") implies that every gap is *O*((log *p*)2), which, while larger than the average gap, is far smaller than the bound implied by the Riemann hypothesis. Numerical evidence supports Cramér's conjecture.[[18]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTENicely1999-18) ### Analytic criteria equivalent to the Riemann hypothesis [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: Analytic criteria equivalent to the Riemann hypothesis\")] Many statements equivalent to the Riemann hypothesis have been found, though so far none of them have led to much progress in proving (or disproving) it. Some typical examples are as follows. (Others involve the [divisor function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divisor_function#Growth_rate \"Divisor function\") *σ*(*n*).) The [Riesz criterion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riesz_criterion \"Riesz criterion\") was given by [Riesz (1916)](#CITEREFRiesz1916), to the effect that the bound : ![{\\displaystyle -\\sum _{k=1}^{\\infty }{\\frac {(-x)^{k}}{(k-1)!\\zeta (2k)}}=O\\left(x^{{\\frac {1}{4}}+\\epsilon }\\right)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f64126def053989510ecf50fa50fbdf85a83d678) holds for all ε > 0 if and only if the Riemann hypothesis holds. See also the [Hardy–Littlewood criterion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riesz_function#Hardy–Littlewood_criterion \"Riesz function\"). [Nyman (1950)](#CITEREFNyman1950) proved that the Riemann hypothesis is true if and only if the space of functions of the form : ![{\\displaystyle f(x)=\\sum _{\\nu =1}^{n}c_{\\nu }\\rho \\left({\\frac {\\theta _{\\nu }}{x}}\\right)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/2ffded3ca297993db53663f6156a6586daac2c9e) where *ρ*(*z*) is the fractional part of *z*, 0 ≤ *θ**ν* ≤ 1, and : ![{\\displaystyle \\sum _{\\nu =1}^{n}c_{\\nu }\\theta _{\\nu }=0,}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/c6e2d97dc055772113a8675a29bf68c383f4439a) is dense in the [Hilbert space *L*2(0,1)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lp_space \"Lp space\") of square-integrable functions on the unit interval. [Beurling (1955)](#CITEREFBeurling1955) extended this by showing that the zeta function has no zeros with real part greater than 1/*p* if and only if this function space is dense in *Lp*(0,1). This Nyman-Beurling criterion was strengthened by Baez-Duarte[[19]](#cite_note-baez-19) to the case where ![{\\displaystyle \\theta _{\\nu }\\in \\{1/k\\}_{k\\geq 1}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/56ae7130d3bec0d1f12198c13a3aa1176dcacf03). [Salem (1953)](#CITEREFSalem1953) showed that the Riemann hypothesis is true if and only if the [integral equation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integral_equation \"Integral equation\") : ![{\\displaystyle \\int _{0}^{\\infty }{\\frac {z^{-\\sigma -1}\\varphi (z)}{{e^{x/z}}+1}}\\,dz=0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/a82945c356b02cc6d44b9f15db9424041245b23a) has no non-trivial bounded solutions ![{\\displaystyle \\varphi }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/33ee699558d09cf9d653f6351f9fda0b2f4aaa3e) for ![{\\displaystyle 1/22}(-1)^{(p+1)/2}x^{p}=+\\infty ,}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87f985ea88bc9a21e500bd4b0c5cbd26ef95bd6d) which says that primes 3 mod 4 are more common than primes 1 mod 4 in some sense. (For related results, see *[Prime number theorem § Prime number race](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number_theorem#Prime_number_race \"Prime number theorem\")*.) * In 1923, Hardy and Littlewood showed that the generalized Riemann hypothesis implies a weak form of the [Goldbach conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldbach_conjecture \"Goldbach conjecture\") for odd numbers: that every sufficiently large odd number is the sum of three primes, though in 1937 Vinogradov gave an unconditional proof. In 1997 [Deshouillers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Marc_Deshouillers \"Jean-Marc Deshouillers\"), Effinger, [te Riele](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herman_te_Riele \"Herman te Riele\"), and Zinoviev showed that the generalized Riemann hypothesis implies that every odd number greater than 5 is the sum of three primes. In 2013 [Harald Helfgott](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harald_Helfgott \"Harald Helfgott\") proved the ternary Goldbach conjecture without the GRH dependence, subject to some extensive calculations completed with the help of David J. Platt. * In 1934, Chowla showed that the generalized Riemann hypothesis implies that the first prime in the arithmetic progression *a* mod *m* is at most *Km*2log(*m*)2 for some fixed constant *K*. * In 1967, Hooley showed that the generalized Riemann hypothesis implies [Artin's conjecture on primitive roots](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artin's_conjecture_on_primitive_roots \"Artin's conjecture on primitive roots\"). * In 1973, Weinberger showed that the generalized Riemann hypothesis implies that Euler's list of [idoneal numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idoneal_number \"Idoneal number\") is complete. * [Weinberger (1973)](#CITEREFWeinberger1973) showed that the generalized Riemann hypothesis for the zeta functions of all algebraic number fields implies that any number field with class number 1 is either [Euclidean](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_domain \"Euclidean domain\") or an imaginary quadratic number field of [discriminant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discriminant_of_an_algebraic_number_field \"Discriminant of an algebraic number field\") −19, −43, −67, or −163. * In 1976, G. Miller showed that the generalized Riemann hypothesis implies that one can [test if a number is prime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primality_test \"Primality test\") in polynomial time via the [Miller test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller–Rabin_primality_test \"Miller–Rabin primality test\"). In 2002, Manindra Agrawal, Neeraj Kayal and Nitin Saxena proved this result unconditionally using the [AKS primality test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test \"AKS primality test\"). * [Odlyzko (1990)](#CITEREFOdlyzko1990) discussed how the generalized Riemann hypothesis can be used to give sharper estimates for discriminants and class numbers of number fields. * [Ono & Soundararajan (1997)](#CITEREFOnoSoundararajan1997) showed that the generalized Riemann hypothesis implies that [Ramanujan's integral quadratic form](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramanujan's_ternary_quadratic_form \"Ramanujan's ternary quadratic form\") *x*2 + *y*2 + 10*z*2 represents all integers that it represents locally, with exactly 18 exceptions. * In 2021, Alexander (Alex) Dunn and [Maksym Radziwill](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maksym_Radziwill \"Maksym Radziwill\") proved [Patterson's conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patterson's_conjecture?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Patterson's conjecture (page does not exist)\") on cubic [Gauss sums](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauss_sums \"Gauss sums\"), under the assumption of the GRH.[[23]](#cite_note-23)[[24]](#cite_note-Dunn_Radziwiłł_2021_Patterson-24) ### Excluded middle [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=10 \"Edit section: Excluded middle\")] Some consequences of the RH are also consequences of its negation, and are thus theorems. In their discussion of the [Hecke, Deuring, Mordell, Heilbronn theorem](#Gauss's_class_number_conjecture), [Ireland & Rosen (1990](#CITEREFIrelandRosen1990), p. 359) say > The method of proof here is truly amazing. If the generalized Riemann hypothesis is true, then the theorem is true. If the generalized Riemann hypothesis is false, then the theorem is true. Thus, the theorem is true!! Care should be taken to understand what is meant by saying the generalized Riemann hypothesis is false: one should specify exactly which class of Dirichlet series has a counterexample. #### Littlewood's theorem [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=11 \"Edit section: Littlewood's theorem\")] This concerns the sign of the error in the [prime number theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number_theorem \"Prime number theorem\"). It has been computed that π(*x*) li(*x*). In 1914, Littlewood proved that there are arbitrarily large values of *x* for which : ![{\\displaystyle \\pi (x)>\\operatorname {li} (x)+{\\frac {1}{3}}{\\frac {\\sqrt {x}}{\\log x}}\\log \\log \\log x,}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7b8233ec67e8879db2e365b4601f71bba6e8801f) and that there are also arbitrarily large values of *x* for which : ![{\\displaystyle \\pi (x)C{\\frac {\\sqrt {|D|}}{\\log |D|}}.}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/d952a15867da8e64956e8a2172ae738ea7d70131) **Theorem (Deuring; 1933)**—If the RH is false then *h*(*D*) > 1 if |*D*| is sufficiently large. **Theorem (Mordell; 1934)**—If the RH is false then *h*(*D*) → ∞ as *D* → −∞. **Theorem (Heilbronn; 1934)**—If the generalized RH is false for the *L*-function of some imaginary quadratic Dirichlet character then *h*(*D*) → ∞ as *D* → −∞. (In the work of Hecke and Heilbronn, the only [*L*-functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L-function \"L-function\") that occur are those attached to imaginary quadratic characters, and it is only for those *L*-functions that *GRH is true* or *GRH is false* is intended; a failure of GRH for the *L*-function of a cubic Dirichlet character would, strictly speaking, mean GRH is false, but that was not the kind of failure of GRH that Heilbronn had in mind, so his assumption was more restricted than simply *GRH is false*.) In 1935, [Carl Siegel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Siegel \"Carl Siegel\") strengthened the result without using RH or GRH in any way.[[26]](#cite_note-Goldfeld_1985_pp._23–37-26)[[27]](#cite_note-Siegel_1935_pp._83–86-27) #### Growth of Euler's totient [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=13 \"Edit section: Growth of Euler's totient\")] In 1983 [J. L. Nicolas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Louis_Nicolas \"Jean-Louis Nicolas\") proved that ![{\\displaystyle \\varphi (n)0,}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/d19e84b13ee72081ccf1b776c455a4f635150270) where λ(*n*) is the [Liouville function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liouville_function \"Liouville function\") given by (−1)*r* if *n* has *r* prime factors.[[36]](#cite_note-36) He showed that this in turn would imply that the Riemann hypothesis is true. But [Haselgrove (1958)](#CITEREFHaselgrove1958) proved that *T*(*x*) is negative for infinitely many *x* (and also disproved the closely related [Pólya conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pólya_conjecture \"Pólya conjecture\")), and [Borwein, Ferguson & Mossinghoff (2008)](#CITEREFBorweinFergusonMossinghoff2008) showed that the smallest such *x* is 72185376951205. [Spira (1968)](#CITEREFSpira1968) showed by numerical calculation that the finite [Dirichlet series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_series \"Dirichlet series\") above for *N* = 19 has a zero with real part greater than 1. Turán also showed that a somewhat weaker assumption, the nonexistence of zeros with real part greater than 1 + *N*−1/2+*ε* for large *N* in the finite Dirichlet series above, would also imply the Riemann hypothesis, but [Montgomery (1983)](#CITEREFMontgomery1983) showed that for all sufficiently large *N* these series have zeros with real part greater than 1 + (log log *N*)/(4 log *N*). Therefore, Turán's result is [vacuously true](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vacuous_truth \"Vacuous truth\") and cannot help prove the Riemann hypothesis. ### Noncommutative geometry [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=26 \"Edit section: Noncommutative geometry\")] [Alain Connes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alain_Connes \"Alain Connes\") has described a relationship between the Riemann hypothesis and [noncommutative geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noncommutative_geometry \"Noncommutative geometry\"), and showed that a suitable analog of the [Selberg trace formula](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selberg_trace_formula \"Selberg trace formula\") for the action of the [idèle class group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idèle_class_group \"Idèle class group\") on the adèle class space would imply the Riemann hypothesis.[[37]](#cite_note-37) Some of these ideas are elaborated in [Lapidus (2008)](#CITEREFLapidus2008). ### Hilbert spaces of entire functions [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=27 \"Edit section: Hilbert spaces of entire functions\")] [Louis de Branges](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_de_Branges_de_Bourcia \"Louis de Branges de Bourcia\") showed that the Riemann hypothesis would follow from a positivity condition on a certain [Hilbert space](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert_space \"Hilbert space\") of [entire functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entire_function \"Entire function\").[[38]](#cite_note-38) However [Conrey & Li (2000)](#CITEREFConreyLi2000) showed that the necessary positivity conditions are not satisfied. Despite this obstacle, de Branges has continued to work on an attempted proof of the Riemann hypothesis along the same lines, but this has not been widely accepted by other mathematicians.[[39]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTESarnak2005-39) ### Quasicrystals [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=28 \"Edit section: Quasicrystals\")] The Riemann hypothesis implies that the zeros of the zeta function form a [quasicrystal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quasicrystal \"Quasicrystal\"), a distribution with discrete support whose [Fourier transform](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourier_transform \"Fourier transform\") also has discrete support. [Dyson (2009)](#CITEREFDyson2009) suggested trying to prove the Riemann hypothesis by classifying, or at least studying, 1-dimensional quasicrystals. ### Arithmetic zeta functions of models of elliptic curves over number fields [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=29 \"Edit section: Arithmetic zeta functions of models of elliptic curves over number fields\")] When one goes from geometric dimension one, e.g. an [algebraic number field](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_number_field \"Algebraic number field\"), to geometric dimension two, e.g. a regular model of an [elliptic curve](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_curve \"Elliptic curve\") over a number field, the two-dimensional part of the generalized Riemann hypothesis for the [arithmetic zeta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arithmetic_zeta_function \"Arithmetic zeta function\") of the model deals with the poles of the zeta function. In dimension one the study of the zeta integral in [Tate's thesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tate's_thesis \"Tate's thesis\") does not lead to new important information on the Riemann hypothesis. Contrary to this, in dimension two work of [Ivan Fesenko](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_Fesenko \"Ivan Fesenko\") on two-dimensional generalisation of Tate's thesis includes an integral representation of a zeta integral closely related to the zeta function. In this new situation, not possible in dimension one, the poles of the zeta function can be studied via the zeta integral and associated adele groups. Related conjecture of [Ivan Fesenko](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_Fesenko \"Ivan Fesenko\") on the positivity of the fourth derivative of a boundary function associated to the zeta integral essentially implies the pole part of the generalized Riemann hypothesis.[[40]](#cite_note-40) Suzuki ([2011](#CITEREFSuzuki2011)) proved that the latter, together with some technical assumptions, implies Fesenko's conjecture. ### Multiple zeta functions [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=30 \"Edit section: Multiple zeta functions\")] Deligne's proof of the Riemann hypothesis over finite fields used the zeta functions of product varieties, whose zeros and poles correspond to sums of zeros and poles of the original zeta function, in order to bound the real parts of the zeros of the original zeta function. By analogy, [Kurokawa (1992)](#CITEREFKurokawa1992) introduced multiple zeta functions whose zeros and poles correspond to sums of zeros and poles of the Riemann zeta function. To make the series converge he restricted to sums of zeros or poles all with non-negative imaginary part. So far, the known bounds on the zeros and poles of the multiple zeta functions are not strong enough to give useful estimates for the zeros of the Riemann zeta function. ## Location of the zeros [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=31 \"Edit section: Location of the zeros\")] ### Number of zeros [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=32 \"Edit section: Number of zeros\")] The functional equation combined with the [argument principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argument_principle \"Argument principle\") implies that the number of zeros of the zeta function with imaginary part between 0 and *T* is given by : ![{\\displaystyle N(T)={\\frac {1}{\\pi }}\\mathop {\\mathrm {Arg} } (\\xi (s))={\\frac {1}{\\pi }}\\mathop {\\mathrm {Arg} } (\\Gamma ({\\tfrac {s}{2}})\\pi ^{-{\\frac {s}{2}}}\\zeta (s)s(s-1)/2)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e6898e859ada0de3d5e0be5e44ca56a21bd10580) for *s* = 1/2 + *iT*, where the argument is defined by varying it continuously along the line with Im(*s*) = *T*, starting with argument 0 at ∞ + *iT*. This is the sum of a large but well understood term : ![{\\displaystyle {\\frac {1}{\\pi }}\\mathop {\\mathrm {Arg} } (\\Gamma ({\\tfrac {s}{2}})\\pi ^{-s/2}s(s-1)/2)={\\frac {T}{2\\pi }}\\log {\\frac {T}{2\\pi }}-{\\frac {T}{2\\pi }}+7/8+O(1/T)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/8e0c219b0cb4246f8fcf09894f106d865172422c) and a small but rather mysterious term : ![{\\displaystyle S(T)={\\frac {1}{\\pi }}\\mathop {\\mathrm {Arg} } (\\zeta (1/2+iT))=O(\\log T).}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/cbb3d13d7fd48fa8151267ce66aed90854dd761c) So the density of zeros with imaginary part near *T* is about log(*T*)/(2π), and the function *S* describes the small deviations from this. The function *S*(*t*) jumps by 1 at each zero of the zeta function, and for *t* ≥ 8 it decreases [monotonically](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monotonically_decreasing \"Monotonically decreasing\") between zeros with derivative close to −log *t*. [Trudgian (2014)](#CITEREFTrudgian2014) proved that, if *T* > *e*, then : ![{\\displaystyle |N(T)-{\\frac {T}{2\\pi }}\\log {\\frac {T}{2\\pi e}}|\\leq 0.112\\log T+0.278\\log \\log T+3.385+{\\frac {0.2}{T}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/c8b1bef6a60a3d757c0410644cfb74f92a21c2b4). [Karatsuba](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatolii_Alexeevitch_Karatsuba \"Anatolii Alexeevitch Karatsuba\") (1996) proved that every interval (*T*, *T* + *H*] for ![{\\displaystyle H\\geq T^{{\\frac {27}{82}}+\\varepsilon }}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/deb900d659d59ff997c06e31da1601b3a66d1ccf) contains at least : ![{\\displaystyle H(\\log T)^{\\frac {1}{3}}e^{-c{\\sqrt {\\log \\log T}}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/db130eb44e39bc37608b3be7a818daf57bd5d084) points where the function *S*(*t*) changes sign. [Selberg (1946)](#CITEREFSelberg1946) showed that the average moments of even powers of *S* are given by : ![{\\displaystyle \\int _{0}^{T}|S(t)|^{2k}dt={\\frac {(2k)!}{k!(2\\pi )^{2k}}}T(\\log \\log T)^{k}+O(T(\\log \\log T)^{k-1/2}).}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/fe755065e201b43888916edee42e9e9f5409b0fe) This suggests that *S*(*T*)/(log log *T*)1/2 resembles a [Gaussian random variable](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaussian_random_variable \"Gaussian random variable\") with mean 0 and variance 2π2 ([Ghosh (1983)](#CITEREFGhosh1983) proved this fact). In particular |*S*(*T*)| is usually somewhere around (log log *T*)1/2, but occasionally much larger. The exact order of growth of *S*(*T*) is not known. There has been no unconditional improvement to Riemann's original bound *S*(*T*) = *O*(log *T*), though the Riemann hypothesis implies the slightly smaller bound *S*(*T*) = *O*(log *T*/log log *T*).[[17]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTETitchmarsh1986-17) The true order of magnitude may be somewhat less than this, as random functions with the same distribution as *S*(*T*) tend to have growth of order about log(*T*)1/2. In the other direction it cannot be too small: [Selberg (1946)](#CITEREFSelberg1946) showed that *S*(*T*) ≠ *o*((log *T*)1/3/(log log *T*)7/3), and assuming the Riemann hypothesis Montgomery showed that *S*(*T*) ≠ *o*((log *T*)1/2/(log log *T*)1/2). Numerical calculations confirm that *S* grows very slowly: |*S*(*T*)| 1, *t* real, and looking at the limit as *σ* → 1. This inequality follows by taking the real part of the log of the Euler product to see that : ![{\\displaystyle |\\zeta (\\sigma +it)|=\\exp \\Re \\sum _{p^{n}}{\\frac {p^{-n(\\sigma +it)}}{n}}=\\exp \\sum _{p^{n}}{\\frac {p^{-n\\sigma }\\cos(t\\log p^{n})}{n}},}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/4270949065294a17ae8553308b54ef1a0bda58ad) where the sum is over all prime powers *p**n*, so that : ![{\\displaystyle |\\zeta (\\sigma )^{3}\\zeta (\\sigma +it)^{4}\\zeta (\\sigma +2it)|=\\exp \\sum _{p^{n}}p^{-n\\sigma }{\\frac {3+4\\cos(t\\log p^{n})+\\cos(2t\\log p^{n})}{n}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/1f0345c68f615920817013f78999f94892f93801) which is at least 1 because all the terms in the sum are positive, due to the inequality : ![{\\displaystyle 3+4\\cos(\\theta )+\\cos(2\\theta )=2(1+\\cos(\\theta ))^{2}\\geq 0.}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/1f6602420aecec7810677ac2f6fd8652ece0a794) ### Zero-free regions [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=34 \"Edit section: Zero-free regions\")] The most extensive computer search by Platt and [Trudgian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Trudgian \"Timothy Trudgian\")[[22]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPlattTrudgian2021-22) for counterexamples of the Riemann hypothesis has verified it for |*t*| ≤ 3.0001753328×1012. Beyond that zero-free regions are known as inequalities concerning *σ* + *i t*, which can be zeroes. The oldest version is from [De la Vallée-Poussin (1899–1900)](#CITEREFde_la_Vallée-Poussin1899–1900), who proved there is a region without zeroes that satisfies 1 − *σ* ≥ ⁠*C*/log(*t*)⁠ for some positive constant *C*. In other words, zeros cannot be too close to the line *σ* = 1: there is a zero-free region close to this line. This has been enlarged by several authors using methods such as [Vinogradov's mean-value theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vinogradov's_mean-value_theorem \"Vinogradov's mean-value theorem\"). The most recent paper[[42]](#cite_note-42) by Mossinghoff, Trudgian and Yang is from December 2022 and provides four zero-free regions that improved the previous results of Kevin Ford from 2002, Mossinghoff and Trudgian themselves from 2015 and Pace Nielsen's slight improvement of Ford from October 2022: : ![{\\displaystyle \\sigma \\geq 1-{\\frac {1}{5.558691\\log |t|}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/2c9bdeb4a73f506c70c49d8108288b0ae8a59ecf) whenever ![{\\displaystyle |t|\\geq 2}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/291fd5823338d8b6d7c04d97864056d4c7a93904), : ![{\\displaystyle \\sigma \\geq 1-{\\frac {1}{55.241(\\log {|t|})^{2/3}(\\log {\\log {|t|}})^{1/3}}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/782f520f5602864fefc8f8c9b5d109cc9e37efea) whenever ![{\\displaystyle |t|\\geq 3}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/68144a838d911b503097d3e2b637ac9732cb116b) (largest known region in the bound ![{\\displaystyle 3.0001753328\\cdot 10^{12}\\leq |t|\\leq \\exp(64.1)\\approx 6.89\\cdot 10^{27}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/ec9fa16c89271039a24af4bf61ce93ffbca650bb)), : ![{\\displaystyle \\sigma \\geq 1-{\\frac {0.04962-{\\frac {0.0196}{1.15+\\log 3+{\\frac {1}{6}}\\log t+\\log \\log t}}}{0.685+\\log 3+{\\frac {1}{6}}\\log t+1.155\\cdot \\log \\log t}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/52f5b1deb68d62b9bd333a2365d0fe4bbb6cd01d) whenever ![{\\displaystyle |t|\\geq 1.88\\cdot 10^{14}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/56fbbf2c92464fff744585f416b0d69b5605dfc3) (largest known region in the bound ![{\\displaystyle \\exp(64.1)\\leq |t|\\leq \\exp(1000)\\approx 1.97\\cdot 10^{434}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/76bfbaa4b66d5e6dd085cfce00839fb7ad220128)) and : ![{\\displaystyle \\sigma \\geq 1-{\\frac {0.05035}{{\\frac {27}{164}}(\\log {|t|})+7.096}}+{\\frac {0.0349}{({\\frac {27}{164}}(\\log {|t|})+7.096)^{2}}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/99b16731b669a7b41bbc9b6dc8479e425a72fb3d) whenever ![{\\displaystyle |t|\\geq \\exp(1000)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/fa06b91461a7d978971019433fb77446ed357feb) (largest known region in its own bound) The paper also presents an improvement to the second zero-free region, whose bounds are unknown on account of ![{\\displaystyle |t|}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/fa9b1439497e4de838a6b1bcf724ef7a8fe48147) being merely assumed to be \"sufficiently large\" to fulfill the requirements of the paper's proof. This region is : ![{\\displaystyle \\sigma \\geq 1-{\\frac {1}{48.1588(\\log {|t|})^{2/3}(\\log {\\log {|t|}})^{1/3}}}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/bdbdec7b7e7fb7830eee6b22dac5aece6d54e2b2). ## Zeros on the critical line [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=35 \"Edit section: Zeros on the critical line\")] [Hardy (1914)](#CITEREFHardy1914) and [Hardy & Littlewood (1921)](#CITEREFHardyLittlewood1921) showed there are infinitely many zeros on the critical line, by considering moments of certain functions related to the zeta function. [Selberg (1942)](#CITEREFSelberg1942) proved that at least a (small) positive proportion of zeros lie on the line. [Levinson (1974)](#CITEREFLevinson1974) improved this to one-third of the zeros by relating the zeros of the zeta function to those of its derivative, and [Conrey (1989)](#CITEREFConrey1989) improved this further to two-fifths. In 2020, this estimate was extended to five-twelfths (i.e., 41.6%) by Pratt, Robles, [Zaharescu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandru_Zaharescu \"Alexandru Zaharescu\") and Zeindler[[43]](#cite_note-43) by considering extended mollifiers that can accommodate higher order derivatives of the zeta function and their associated Kloosterman sums. In 2026, AI company [Anthropic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthropic \"Anthropic\") claimed that their [Claude](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_(AI) \"Claude (AI)\") model succeeded in improving the lower bound to 67.2% using 60 subagents. [[44]](#cite_note-44) Most zeros lie close to the critical line. More precisely, [Bohr & Landau (1914)](#CITEREFBohrLandau1914) showed that for any positive *ε*, the number of zeros with real part at least 1/2+*ε* and imaginary part at between −*T* and *T* is ![{\\displaystyle O(T)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/ac1c627e228735c52500a419a6b4af4ed02a1574). Combined with the facts that zeros on the critical strip are symmetric about the critical line and that the total number of zeros in the critical strip is ![{\\displaystyle \\Theta (T\\log T)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/8917ac6a6c139f9f59a03f99dcfa83499b2c6981), [almost all](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Almost_all \"Almost all\") non-trivial zeros are within a distance *ε* of the critical line. [Ivić (1985)](#CITEREFIvić1985) gives several more precise versions of this result, called *zero density estimates*, which bound the number of zeros in regions with imaginary part at most *T* and real part at least 1/2 + *ε*. ### Hardy–Littlewood conjectures [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=36 \"Edit section: Hardy–Littlewood conjectures\")] In 1914 [Godfrey Harold Hardy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G._H._Hardy \"G. H. Hardy\") proved that ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta \\left({\\tfrac {1}{2}}+it\\right)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/928fbdc88de8d575f6aa4f27efbd48fafc031a7c) has infinitely many real zeros. The next two conjectures of [Hardy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G._H._Hardy \"G. H. Hardy\") and [John Edensor Littlewood](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Edensor_Littlewood \"John Edensor Littlewood\") on the distance between real zeros of ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta \\left({\\tfrac {1}{2}}+it\\right)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/928fbdc88de8d575f6aa4f27efbd48fafc031a7c) and on the density of zeros of ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta \\left({\\tfrac {1}{2}}+it\\right)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/928fbdc88de8d575f6aa4f27efbd48fafc031a7c) on the interval ![{\\displaystyle (T,T+H]}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/5a7f206ba185f5e71e82482b52823f9cab4a9d99) for sufficiently large ![{\\displaystyle T>0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e39e90b9e1e7d5be3b5eae57729dc63494bbe3fd), and ![{\\displaystyle H=T^{a+\\varepsilon }}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7b04fad3c3d01d0dc45e856f05d271dc583aebd0) and with as small as possible value of ![{\\displaystyle a>0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/1f34a80ea013edb56e340b19550430a8b6dfd7b9), where ![{\\displaystyle \\varepsilon >0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e04ec3670b50384a3ce48aca42e7cc5131a06b12) is an arbitrarily small number, open two new directions in the investigation of the Riemann zeta function: 1. For any ![{\\displaystyle \\varepsilon >0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e04ec3670b50384a3ce48aca42e7cc5131a06b12) there exists a lower bound ![{\\displaystyle T_{0}=T_{0}(\\varepsilon )>0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f6d6537b59504874ae1259697e665ff90c2af4a7) such that for ![{\\displaystyle T\\geq T_{0}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/0ffb4a83f66b6423208845cbd53c34044acecea2) and ![{\\displaystyle H=T^{{\\tfrac {1}{4}}+\\varepsilon }}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/428c2185ec1ddf2d9471845ba74f7650b78ea2d3) the interval ![{\\displaystyle (T,T+H]}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/5a7f206ba185f5e71e82482b52823f9cab4a9d99) contains a zero of odd order of the function ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta {\\bigl (}{\\tfrac {1}{2}}+it{\\bigr )}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b693149f8a701807569e4ad91ae936bebcb30373). Let ![{\\displaystyle N(T)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/891f08bfa8e782f2db0a06b00aa11668b1614537) be the total number of real zeros, and ![{\\displaystyle N_{0}(T)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/61065a59d53db2b19b928cb09813c3f744cf2e68) be the total number of zeros of odd order of the function ![{\\displaystyle ~\\zeta \\left({\\tfrac {1}{2}}+it\\right)~}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/674a28fdaf3403f4f61868a028614e45d5c00918) lying on the interval ![{\\displaystyle (0,T]~}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/32185e31a7ceddedfbd8767e0dd79a6afbb0582c). 1. For any ![{\\displaystyle \\varepsilon >0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e04ec3670b50384a3ce48aca42e7cc5131a06b12) there exists ![{\\displaystyle T_{0}=T_{0}(\\varepsilon )>0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f6d6537b59504874ae1259697e665ff90c2af4a7) and some ![{\\displaystyle c=c(\\varepsilon )>0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/18530fcafc8a7bbfe84b253fdf94d59d04bf3bf4), such that for ![{\\displaystyle T\\geq T_{0}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/0ffb4a83f66b6423208845cbd53c34044acecea2) and ![{\\displaystyle H=T^{{\\tfrac {1}{2}}+\\varepsilon }}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f5d5d48a2b68012f86ce5fc1ae503c8d45363b54) the inequality ![{\\displaystyle N_{0}(T+H)-N_{0}(T)\\geq cH}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/8fd2447ff5cffa6cfa95ced90f59c0de7d096362) is true. ### Selberg's zeta function conjecture [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=37 \"Edit section: Selberg's zeta function conjecture\")] Main article: [Selberg's zeta function conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selberg's_zeta_function_conjecture \"Selberg's zeta function conjecture\") [Atle Selberg](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atle_Selberg \"Atle Selberg\") investigated the problem of Hardy–Littlewood *2* and proved that for any *ε* > 0 there exists such ![{\\displaystyle T_{0}=T_{0}(\\varepsilon )>0}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f6d6537b59504874ae1259697e665ff90c2af4a7) and *c* = *c*(*ε*) > 0, such that for ![{\\displaystyle T\\geq T_{0}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/0ffb4a83f66b6423208845cbd53c34044acecea2) and ![{\\displaystyle H=T^{0.5+\\varepsilon }}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/008dfbf7fe78bb0ad6c2f9448b45d14aaeea6fb5) the inequality ![{\\displaystyle N(T+H)-N(T)\\geq cH\\log T}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/5dfd594d5f0550aa1c343110af08ffbe978e7b0a) is true.[[45]](#cite_note-45) Selberg conjectured that this could be tightened to ![{\\displaystyle H=T^{0.5}}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/8eedf31cf1202cc12358d0ba9168a014189fdfb3). [Anatoly Karatsuba](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatoly_Karatsuba \"Anatoly Karatsuba\") proved that for a fixed *ε* satisfying the condition 0 0, the [partial sums](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Partial_sum \"Partial sum\") ![{\\displaystyle M(x)=\\sum _{n\\leq x}\\mu (n)}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/056958331c498fd0fdb9b4b76c1101a97f352d0b) (the values of which are positions in a [simple random walk](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simple_random_walk \"Simple random walk\")) satisfy the bound ![{\\displaystyle M(x)=O(x^{1/2+\\varepsilon })}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/2c75c3dd2aa902d62979d1d0546b7d811daca627) with [probability 1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Almost_surely \"Almost surely\"). The Riemann hypothesis is equivalent to this bound for the [Möbius function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Möbius_function \"Möbius function\") μ and the [Mertens function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mertens_function \"Mertens function\") *M* derived in the same way from it. In other words, the Riemann hypothesis is in some sense equivalent to saying that *μ*(*x*) behaves like a random sequence of coin tosses. When *μ*(*x*) is nonzero its sign gives the [parity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parity_(mathematics) \"Parity (mathematics)\") of the number of prime factors of *x*, so informally the Riemann hypothesis says that the parity of the number of prime factors of an integer behaves randomly. Such probabilistic arguments in number theory often give the right answer, but tend to be very hard to make rigorous, and occasionally give the wrong answer for some results, such as [Maier's theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maier's_theorem \"Maier's theorem\"). * The calculations in [Odlyzko (1987)](#CITEREFOdlyzko1987) show that the zeros of the zeta function behave very much like the eigenvalues of a random [Hermitian matrix](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermitian_matrix \"Hermitian matrix\"), suggesting that they are the eigenvalues of some self-adjoint operator, which would imply the Riemann hypothesis. All attempts to find such an operator have failed. * There are several theorems, such as [Goldbach's weak conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldbach's_weak_conjecture \"Goldbach's weak conjecture\") for sufficiently large odd numbers, that were first proved using the generalized Riemann hypothesis, and later shown to be true unconditionally. This could be considered as weak evidence for the generalized Riemann hypothesis, as several of its \"predictions\" are true. * [Lehmer's phenomenon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lehmer_pair \"Lehmer pair\"),[[69]](#cite_note-FOOTNOTELehmer1956-69) where two zeros are sometimes very close, is sometimes given as a reason to disbelieve the Riemann hypothesis. But one would expect this to happen occasionally by chance even if the Riemann hypothesis is true, and Odlyzko's calculations suggest that nearby pairs of zeros occur just as often as predicted by [Montgomery's conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montgomery's_pair_correlation_conjecture \"Montgomery's pair correlation conjecture\"). * [Patterson](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_James_Patterson \"Samuel James Patterson\") suggests that the most compelling reason for the Riemann hypothesis for most mathematicians is the hope that primes are distributed as regularly as possible.[[70]](#cite_note-70) ## Notes [[edit](/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=42 \"Edit section: Notes\")] 1. [↑](#cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBombieri2000_1-0) [Bombieri (2000)](#CITEREFBombieri2000). 2. [↑](#cite_ref-2) Bernhard Riemann ([1859](#CITEREFRiemann1859)) 3. [↑](#cite_ref-3) [Connes 2026](#CITEREFConnes2026) 4. [↑](#cite_ref-4) Euler, Leonhard (1744). [Variae observationes circa series infinitas.](http://eulerarchive.maa.org/docs/originals/E072.pdf) *Commentarii academiae scientiarum Petropolitanae* 9, pp. 160–188, Theorems 7 and 8. In Theorem 7 Euler proves the formula in the special case ![{\\displaystyle s=1}](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/bac386d8f227fb823cede9b3e33d706cad3ed306), and in Theorem 8 he proves it more generally. In the first corollary to his Theorem 7 he notes that ![{\\displaystyle \\zeta (1)=\\log \\infty }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/748ce80df13c8038942ac56f3d36acf3ae33f813), and he makes use of this latter result in his Theorem 19, to show that the sum of the inverses of the prime numbers is ![{\\displaystyle \\log \\log \\infty }](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/78628b1057eec5b4ab5eaf997893a399563f88ac). 5. [↑](#cite_ref-5) Values for *ζ* can be found by calculating, e.g., *ζ*(1/2 − 30*i*). 6. [↑](#cite_ref-6) [Ingham (1932)](#CITEREFIngham1932), Theorem 30, p. 83; [Montgomery & Vaughan (2007)](#CITEREFMontgomeryVaughan2007), p. 430. 7. [↑](#cite_ref-FOOTNOTEIngham193282_7-0) [Ingham (1932)](#CITEREFIngham1932), p. 82. 8. [↑](#cite_ref-8) von Koch, Niels Helge (1901), [\"Sur la distribution des nombres premiers\"](https://zenodo.org/records/2347595), *Acta Mathematica*, **24**: 159–182, [doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier) \"Doi (identifier)\"):[10.1007/BF02403071](https://doi.org/10.1007%2FBF02403071), [S2CID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S2CID_(identifier) \"S2CID (identifier)\") [119914826](https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:119914826) 9. [↑](#cite_ref-9) Dudek, Adrian W. (2014), \"On the Riemann hypothesis and the difference between primes\", *International Journal of Number Theory*, **11** (3): 771–778, [arXiv](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ArXiv_(identifier) \"ArXiv (identifier)\"):[1402.6417](https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.6417), [Bibcode](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibcode_(identifier) \"Bibcode (identifier)\"):[2014arXiv1402.6417D](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2014arXiv1402.6417D), [doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier) \"Doi (identifier)\"):[10.1142/S1793042115500426](https://doi.org/10.1142%2FS1793042115500426), [ISSN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISSN_(identifier) \"ISSN (identifier)\") [1793-0421](https://search.worldcat.org/issn/1793-0421), [S2CID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S2CID_(identifier) \"S2CID (identifier)\") [119321107](https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:119321107) 10. [↑](#cite_ref-10) [Landau, Edmund](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_Landau \"Edmund Landau\") (1924), \"Über die Möbiussche Funktion\", *Rend. Circ. Mat. Palermo*, **48** (2): 277–280, [doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier) \"Doi (identifier)\"):[10.1007/BF03014702](https://doi.org/10.1007%2FBF03014702), [S2CID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S2CID_(identifier) \"S2CID (identifier)\") [123636883](https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:123636883) 11. [↑](#cite_ref-11) Titchmarsh, Edw