)\n\n### 3. The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\n- **URL**: https://arxiv.org/html/2602.04022v1\n- ##### Report GitHub Issue Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: ![](/static/base/1.0.1/images/icons/smileybones-small.svg) ![arXiv logo](/static/base/1.0.1/images/arxiv-logo-primary-light.svg) # The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time ###### Abstract This paper, commissioned as a survey of the Riemann Hypothesis, provides a comprehensive overview of 165 years of mathematical approaches to this fundamental problem, while introducing a new perspective that emerged during its preparation. The paper begins with a detailed description of what we know about the Riemann zeta function and its zeros, followed by an extensive survey of mathematical theories developed in pursuit of RH—from classical analytic approaches to modern geometric and physical methods. We also discuss several equivalent formulations of the hypothesis. Within this survey framework, we present an original contribution in the form of a \"Letter to Riemann,\" using only mathematics available in his time. This letter reveals a method inspired by Riemann’s own approach to the conformal mapping theorem: by extremizing a quadratic form (restriction of Weil’s quadratic form in modern language), we obtain remarkable approximations to the zeros of zeta. Using only primes less than 13, this optimization procedure yields approximations to the first 50 zeros with accuracies ranging from 2.6×10−552.6\\times 10^{-55} to 10−310^{-3}. Moreover we prove a general result that these approximating values lie exactly on the critical line ℜ⁡(z)=12\\Re(z)=\\frac{1}{2}. Following the letter, we explain the underlying mathematics in modern terms, including the description of a deep connection of the Weil quadratic form with the world of information theory. The final sections develop a geometric perspective using trace formulas, outlining a potential proof strategy based on establishing convergence of zeros from finite to infinite Euler products. While completing the commissioned survey, these new results suggest a promising direction for future research on Riemann’s conjecture. ###### Contents ## 1 Introduction The Riemann Hypothesis (RH) stands as perhaps the most famous unsolved problem in mathematics, having resisted all attempts at proof since Bernhard Riemann casually remarked in his 1859 memoir that \"sehr wahrscheinlich\" (very probably) all nontrivial zeros of his zeta function lie on the critical line ℜ⁡(s)=1/2\\Re(s)=1/2. The essence of this paper is a three pages letter to Riemann, written as if it were possible to communicate across the years, and in which I will present a strategy (towards a proof of RH) which only uses mathematics that he was familiar with, together with the great power of modern computers. In the section \"Encounter with the Riemann zeta function\" we aim to convey to the reader a vivid picture of the extraordinary connection that Riemann unveiled between the complex zeros of the zeta function and the distribution of prime numbers. We begin with a short historical account of the prime number theorem, followed by Riemann’s explicit formula for π⁡(x)\\pi(x), the number of primes below xx, and its later generalizations into the explicit formulas. From there we turn to one of the most striking consequences: Littlewood’s discovery that the difference π⁡(x)−Li⁡(x)\\pi(x)-\\mathrm{Li}(x) changes sign infinitely often, an observation that overturned earlier expectations of a simple monotone law. We then trace the story of what has been established about the zeros of ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s), beginning with Hardy’s proof of their infinitude on the critical line, through Selberg’s breakthrough in 1942, and up to modern results concerning the proportion of zeros lying exactly on the critical line. To illuminate this theory, we also draw on the rich framework of entire functions, including Hadamard’s factorization theorem, Nevanlinna theory, and the remarkable Borchsenius–Jessen theorem, itself a success of the Bohr–Landau program on almost periodic functions. This part culminates with a result that would surely have amazed Riemann : Voronin’s universality theorem. It asserts that the zeta function, in the strip 1/20RH\\iff\\det(R\\_{n})=O(n^{1/2+\\epsilon}),\\ \\ \\forall\\epsilon>0 | | These are alluring but treacherous: the history of the subject shows how easily an attempt to solve the problem can be drawn into the gravitational pull of this “black hole” of equivalences. I will discuss two of these equivalent formulations, first Weil’s positivity criterion, then Robin’s criterion as improved by Lagarias. This latter criterion shows that RH belongs to the type of statements that Hilbert, in his program on the foundations of Mathematics, was hoping to be \"provable if true\". But Godel’s theorem showed that precisely for this type of statement truth does not imply provability. This will be discussed in details involving Chaitin’s theory of algorithmic complexity. The next part of this paper, entitled “A Letter to Professor Bernhard Riemann”, is of a completely different nature. It is written in the form of a letter addressed to Riemann himself, in an imaginary dialogue across time. The purpose of the letter is to point out a simple but surprising observation: a strategy towards RH which fits entirely within Riemann’s own mathematical outlook, as exemplified for instance by his proof of the conformal mapping theorem. Importantly, this strategy requires only the mathematical concepts and tools available in 1859. Concretely, it allows one to recover the first nontrivial zeros of the zeta function by using only a few factors from the Euler product. For example, truncating the Euler product at the prime 1313 and computing with the method described, one obtains an approximation of the first 50 zeros of ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s) whose precision is best understood by giving the probability that one could get such an approximation by chance. The likelihood that such agreement could occur merely *by chance* is about 10−123510^{-1235}. To give a sense of scale, this is approximately the probability of correctly guessing the outcomes of over 4000 consecutive coin tosses — a feat so improbable that, for all practical purposes, it excludes both coincidence and computational error. One might therefore be tempted to conclude: ‘‘This is simply a new algorithm for computing the zeros of the Riemann zeta function.’’ Were that the case, the Riemann Hypothesis itself would follow, since a general theorem ensures that whenever the smallest eigenvalue of the corresponding operator is simple and even11 1 meaning that the associated eigenfunction is even, the resulting approximating numbers form the spectrum of a self-adjoint operator and hence are all real. This letter to Riemann is followed, in the section The Next Small Steps, by a more detailed discussion of its content, together with an outline of a possible strategy to justify rigorously that, in general, the approximating zeros produced by this method converge to the actual zeros of the Riemann zeta function. While the numerical evidence in support of this is overwhelming, evidence alone is not a proof. In this natural line of attack, a central role is played by the prolate spheroidal wave functions, which I had introduced into this context in 1998. Computer experiments reveal a striking fact: when expressed in terms of spheroidal wave functions, one obtains an excellent approximation to the function that minimizes the Weil quadratic form. This observation provides strong motivation for the approach. It is of course possible that a complete proof along these lines may encounter serious obstacles. Yet regardless of how far one can progress, this direction naturally opens the way to a deeper exploration, whose starting point is presented in the section Geometric Perspectives — namely, the unexpected relation between two seemingly distant mathematical worlds. On the one hand, there is the world of the Weil quadratic form. The central fact here is Weil’s remarkable equivalence: the Riemann Hypothesis is equivalent to the positivity of certain quadratic forms which involve only finitely many primes. This is striking, since one might expect that addressing the Riemann Hypothesis would require controlling the full infinity of primes. Here, however, the problem reduces locally, to finite collections at a time. Moreover, in this setting, as mentioned earlier, one can exploit the general construction of functions whose zeros lie entirely on the critical line. On the other hand, there is the world of the prolate wave functions developed by David Slepian and collaborators, with roots in Claude Shannon’s work in communication theory. The key link, established in the seminal work of Slepian, Landau, and Pollak, is a classical second-order differential operator on the line: the prolate operator, obtained as a confluence from the Heun equation—something entirely familiar to Riemann’s mathematical universe. This operator plays a dual role. In the infrared regime, it provides a means of approximating the minimal eigenvector of the Weil quadratic form. At the other extreme, as explained in the final section of this paper, it also supplies a model for a self-adjoint operator whose spectrum reflects the ultraviolet behavior of the zeros of the Riemann zeta function. Taken together, these observations underline the importance of understanding situations where only finitely many primes are involved, and of clarifying the interplay between the Weil quadratic form and the prolate operator. This connection is established through the trace formula, which I proved in 1998. In the last section, I will reformulate this trace formula using precisely the same ingredients that arise in communication theory and in the work of Slepian and collaborators, thereby making explicit the bridge it provides between the Weil quadratic form and the prolate functions. Behind the scene there lies a geometric space of fundamental importance: the *semilocal adele class space* YSY\\_{S}, associated with a finite set SS of places of ℚ{\\mathbb{Q}} that includes the archimedean place. By construction, YSY\\_{S} is obtained as the quotient | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | ∏v∈Sℚv/Γ,Γ:={±∏pvnv∣nv∈ℤ}\\prod\\_{v\\in S}{\\mathbb{Q}}\\_{v}/\\Gamma,\\ \\ \\Gamma:=\\{\\pm\\prod p\\_{v}^{n\\_{v}}\\mid n\\_{v}\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}\\} | | of the finite product of local fields by the diagonal action of the group Γ⊂ℚ∗\\Gamma\\subset{\\mathbb{Q}}^{\\*} generated by the primes in SS. Weil’s positivity criterion shows that proving the required positivity in this semilocal setting is equivalent to establishing the Riemann Hypothesis (in fact, one also captures the case of LL-functions with Grössencharacters). A key advantage of this semilocal space, when compared with the full adele class space, is measure-theoretic: here the multiplicative and additive Haar measures are no longer singular with respect to one another, so the quotient behaves well from the viewpoint of measure theory. Topologically, however, the situation is more subtle. For each prime p∈Sp\\in S one encounters a periodic orbit of length log⁡p\\log p, and these orbits encode precisely the contribution of pp to the explicit formula. Finally in §[7.6](#S7.SS6 \"7.6 The prolate wave operator ‣ 7 Geometric Perspectives ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\"), I explain the recent discovery in our joint work with H. Moscovici [[27](#bib.bib27)], where we show that the eigenvalues of the selfadjoint extension of the prolate spheroidal operator reproduce the ultraviolet behavior of the squares of zeros of the Riemann zeta function and construct an isospectral family of Dirac operators whose spectra have the same ultraviolet behavior as the zeros of zeta. ## 2 Encounter with the Riemann zeta function ### 2.1 Classical Analytic Number Theory #### 2.1.1 Prime Number Theorem (PNT): In 1852, Pafnuty Chebyshev, proved in [[14](#bib.bib14)] that the number π⁡(x)\\pi(x) of primes less than xx fulfills | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | A​xlog⁡(x)1\\operatorname{Re}(s)>1, and if f⁡(s)−c/(s−1)f(s)-c/(s-1) has a continuous extension to Re⁡(s)≥1\\operatorname{Re}(s)\\geq 1 for some constant c≥0c\\geq 0, then limx→∞e−x​A​(x)=c.\\lim\\_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}e^{-x}A(x)=c. This result provided exactly the framework needed to convert information about the zeta function’s non-vanishing on Re⁡(s)=1\\operatorname{Re}(s)=1 into precise asymptotic statements about prime-counting functions. The application to the Prime Number Theorem involves expressing the logarithmic derivative of the zeta function as a Mellin transform of the Chebyshev function ψ⁡(x)=∑pk≤xlog⁡p\\psi(x)=\\sum\\_{p^{k}\\leq x}\\log p i.e. the equality −ζ′(s)/ζ(s)=s∫1∞ψ(x)x−(s+1)dx-\\zeta^{\\prime}(s)/\\zeta(s)=s\\int\\_{1}^{\\infty}\\psi(x)x^{-(s+1)}dx. The non-vanishing of ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s) on Re⁡(s)=1\\operatorname{Re}(s)=1, combined with the known behavior of ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s) at s=1s=1, ensures that ζ′​(s)/ζ⁡(s)+1/(s−1)\\zeta^{\\prime}(s)/\\zeta(s)+1/(s-1) has the required analytic properties for applying the Wiener-Ikehara theorem. The theorem then directly implies that ψ⁡(x)∼x\\psi(x)\\sim x, from which the Prime Number Theorem follows through elementary arguments to Newman’s simplified presentation shows how mathematical understanding can deepen through the development of more general and conceptually transparent frameworks. The 1949 elementary proof of the PNT by Selberg and Erdos showed that complex analysis is not logically necessary for proving the theorem. The Selberg sieve, while developed for his elementary proof of PNT [[97](#bib.bib97)], became a fundamental tool in analytic number theory. The Selberg symmetry formula | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | ∑p≤xlog2⁡p+∑p​q≤xlog⁡p​log⁡q=2​x​log⁡x+O⁡(x)\\sum\\_{p\\leq x}\\log^{2}p+\\sum\\_{pq\\leq x}\\log p\\log q=2x\\log x+O(x) | | exemplifies his ability to find unexpected simplicities in prime distribution. #### 2.1.2 Riemann’s formula, von Mangoldt paper In his fundamental paper22 2 which he sent to Chebyshev [[90](#bib.bib90)], Riemann was very careful to define which branch of the integral logarithm he was using and to state the conditional convergence33 3 He was a master of conditional convergence and gave the first rigorous proof that conditionally convergent series can be rearranged to converge to any prescribed value or to diverge entirely. Riemann’s rearrangement theorem emerged from his 1854 habilitation thesis ”Über die Darstellbarkeit einer Function durch eine trigonometrische Reihe”, a work primarily focused on extending the theory of Fourier series to more general classes of functions. Published posthumously in 1867, [[91](#bib.bib91)], through Dedekind’s editorial efforts. in his formula for the function f⁡(x)f(x) obtained as the sum of the counting functions π⁡(x1/n)\\pi(x^{1/n}) | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | f⁡(x)=Li⁡(x)−∑α(Li⁡(x12+α​i)+Li⁡(x12−α​i))+∫x∞1t2−1​d​tt​log⁡t−log⁡2f(x)=\\operatorname{Li}(x)-\\sum\\_{\\alpha}\\left(\\operatorname{Li}\\left(x^{\\frac{1}{2}+\\alpha i}\\right)+\\operatorname{Li}\\left(x^{\\frac{1}{2}-\\alpha i}\\right)\\right)+\\int\\_{x}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{t^{2}-1}\\frac{dt}{t\\log t}-\\log 2 | | (1) | where the 12+α​i\\frac{1}{2}+\\alpha\\ i are the non-trivial zeros of ζ\\zeta with positive imaginary part and the order of the terms corresponds to increasing values of ℜ⁡(α)\\Re(\\alpha). As I will briefly mention at the beginning of my letter to Riemann, it is unfortunate that the care about the various branches of the integral logarithm has disappeared in some modern treatise such as the classic book of Edwards [[44](#bib.bib44)], or in some technical papers, where ([1](#S2.E1 \"In 2.1.2 Riemann’s formula, von Mangoldt paper ‣ 2.1 Classical Analytic Number Theory ‣ 2 Encounter with the Riemann zeta function ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\")) is written as above while there is no function L​i​(z)Li(z) or L​i​(z)+L​i​(z)¯Li(z)+\\overline{Li(z)} for which the general term of the series tends to 00. The problem is with the notation since if one follows Riemann closely one finds that the value he considers depends not just on xρx^{\\rho} but upon ρ​log⁡(x)\\rho\\log(x). In fact in the paper of von Mangoldt [[107](#bib.bib107)], he correctly defines the meaning that he gives, not to the function L​i​(z)Li(z) but to the function L​i​(ew)Li\\left(e^{w}\\right). Here is what he writes: Setting w=u+i​vw=u+iv where u and v denote real numbers, and again understanding hh as a positive real variable, one only needs to make the following definitions: 1. If v>0v>0 , then | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | L​i​(ew)=limh→∞∫−h+wwezz​𝑑z+i​πLi\\left(e^{w}\\right)=\\lim\\_{h\\to\\infty}\\int\\_{-h+w}^{w}\\frac{e^{z}}{z}dz+i\\pi | | 2. If v=0v=0, then | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | L​i​(ew)=limh→0∫−∞−hezz​𝑑z+∫hwezz​𝑑zLi\\left(e^{w}\\right)=\\lim\\_{h\\to 0}\\int\\_{-\\infty}^{-h}\\frac{e^{z}}{z}dz+\\int\\_{h}^{w}\\frac{e^{z}}{z}dz | | 3. If v0\\delta>0 one has f⁡(x)=O⁡(xδ),for​x→0+,f⁡(x)=O⁡(x−1−δ),for​x→+∞.f(x)=O(x^{\\delta}),\\ \\text{for}\\ x\\to 0+,\\ \\ f(x)=O(x^{-1-\\delta}),\\ \\text{for}\\ x\\to+\\infty. , and their Mellin transform defined as | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | f~​(s):=∫0∞f⁡(x)​xs−1​𝑑x\\tilde{f}(s):=\\int\\_{0}^{\\infty}f(x)x^{s-1}dx | | (2) | They take the form | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | ∑ρf~​(ρ)=f~​(0)+f~​(1)−∑v𝒲v​(f),\\sum\\_{\\rho}\\tilde{f}(\\rho)=\\tilde{f}(0)+\\tilde{f}(1)-\\sum\\_{v}{\\mathcal{W}}\\_{v}(f), | | (3) | where the sum on the left hand side is over all complex zeros ρ\\rho of the Riemann zeta function, and is in general only conditionally convergent while the sum on the right hand side runs over all rational places vv of ℚ{\\mathbb{Q}}. The non-archimedean distributions 𝒲p\\mathcal{W}\\_{p} are defined, using f♯​(x):=x−1​f​(x−1)f^{\\sharp}(x):=x^{-1}f(x^{-1}), as | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | 𝒲p​(f):=(log⁡p)​∑m=1∞(f⁡(pm)+f♯​(pm)){\\mathcal{W}}\\_{p}(f):=(\\log p)\\sum\\_{m=1}^{\\infty}\\left(f(p^{m})+f^{\\sharp}(p^{m})\\right) | | (4) | while the archimedean distribution is given by | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | 𝒲ℝ​(f):=(log⁡4​π+γ)​f​(1)+∫1∞(f⁡(x)+f♯​(x)−2x​f​(1))​d​xx−x−1.{\\mathcal{W}}\\_{\\mathbb{R}}(f):=(\\log 4\\pi+\\gamma)f(1)+\\int\\_{1}^{\\infty}\\left(f(x)+f^{\\sharp}(x)-\\frac{2}{x}f(1)\\right)\\frac{dx}{x-x^{-1}}. | | (5) | #### 2.1.4 Changes of sign of π⁡(x)−Li⁡(x)\\pi(x)-\\mathrm{Li}(x) Littlewood’s famous 1914 result [[75](#bib.bib75)] that π⁡(x)−Li⁡(x)\\pi(x)-\\mathrm{Li}(x) changes sign infinitely often (assuming RH) demonstrated that the error term in the PNT exhibits complex oscillatory behavior, challenging the empirical observation that π⁡(x)0\\epsilon>0. If σ\\sigma is real, then μ⁡(σ)\\mu(\\sigma) is defined to be the infimum of all real numbers α\\alpha such that |ζ⁡(σ+i​t)|=O⁡(tα)|\\zeta(\\sigma+it)|=\\mathrm{O}\\left(t^{\\alpha}\\right). One has μ⁡(σ)=0\\mu(\\sigma)=0 for σ>1\\sigma>1, and the functional equation implies that μ⁡(σ)=μ⁡(1−σ)−σ+1/2\\mu(\\sigma)=\\mu(1-\\sigma)-\\sigma+1/2. The Phragmén-Lindelöf theorem implies that μ\\mu is a convex function. The Lindelöf hypothesis states μ⁡(1/2)=0\\mu(1/2)=0, which together with the above properties of μ\\mu implies that μ⁡(σ)\\mu(\\sigma) is 0 for σ≥1/2\\sigma\\geq 1/2 and 1/2−σ1/2-\\sigma for σ≤1/2\\sigma\\leq 1/2. While weaker than RH, it would still have significant applications to moments of LL-functions and subconvexity problems. ### 2.2 Theory of Entire and Meromorphic Functions #### 2.2.1 Hadamard factorization theorem An entire function ff has order σ\\sigma if | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | σ=lim supr→∞log⁡log​M​(r)log⁡r\\sigma=\\limsup\\_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\log\\log M(r)}{\\log r} | | where M⁡(r)=max|z|=r⁡|f⁡(z)|M(r)=\\max\\_{|z|=r}|f(z)|. The Hadamard product formula states that an entire function ff of order 1 can be written as | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | f⁡(z)=eA+B​z​∏ρ(1−zρ)​ez/ρf(z)=e^{A+Bz}\\prod\\_{\\rho}\\left(1-\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)e^{z/\\rho} | | where the product is over all zeros ρ\\rho of ff, and AA, BB are constants. The completed zeta function | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | ξ(s)=12s(s−1)π−s/2Γ(s/2)ζ(s)\\xi(s)=\\frac{1}{2}s(s-1)\\pi^{-s/2}\\Gamma(s/2)\\zeta(s) | | (6) | is an entire function of order one with the Hadamard product representation66 6 The infinite product is defined here as the limit as T→∞T\\to\\infty of the product over ρ\\rho with |ρ|0(1−s2α2)\\Xi(s)=\\Xi(0)\\prod\\_{\\Re(\\alpha)>0}\\left(1-\\frac{s^{2}}{\\alpha^{2}}\\right) | | where the product is over zeros with positive real part only. The two product representations are the same77 7 using the identity 1−12+i​s12+i​a=1−sa1−i2​a1-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}+is}{\\frac{1}{2}+ia}=\\frac{1-\\frac{s}{a}}{1-\\frac{i}{2a}} except for the constant factor and while ξ⁡(0)=12\\xi(0)=\\frac{1}{2}, one has | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | Ξ⁡(0)=−ζ⁡(12)​Γ​(54)2​π4∼0.497121\\Xi(0)=-\\frac{\\zeta\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\Gamma\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}{2\\sqrt[4]{\\pi}}\\sim 0.497121 | | This nuance is at the origin of the only \"typo\" in Riemann’s formula for the number of primes less than xx, where he used log⁡Ξ⁡(0)\\log\\Xi(0) in place of log⁡ξ⁡(0)\\log\\xi(0). #### 2.2.2 Nevanlinna theory This value-distribution theory [[82](#bib.bib82), [83](#bib.bib83), [68](#bib.bib68)] studies the growth and value distribution of meromorphic functions through characteristic functions and deficiency relations. It is worth noting that the Nevanlinna characteristic is closely related to Jensen’s formula. For an analytic function ff with zeros aka\\_{k} in |z|≤r|z|\\leq r, Jensen’s formula asserts that, provided f⁡(0)≠0f(0)\\neq 0, | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | log⁡|f⁡(0)|+∑klog⁡r|ak|=12​π​∫02​πlog⁡|f⁡(r​ei​θ)|​𝑑θ.\\log|f(0)|+\\sum\\_{k}\\log\\frac{r}{|a\\_{k}|}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int\\_{0}^{2\\pi}\\log|f(re^{i\\theta})|\\,d\\theta. | | This relation between the average boundary growth of ff and the distribution of its zeros is precisely what Nevanlinna theory systematizes for meromorphic functions. The characteristic T⁡(r,f)=m⁡(r,f)+N⁡(r,f),r>0,T(r,f)=m(r,f)+N(r,f),r>0, can thus be viewed as a refined version of the quantities appearing in Jensen’s formula, with the counting function N⁡(r,f)N(r,f) recording the poles and the proximity function m⁡(r,f)m(r,f) recording the boundary growth. For the zeta function, one can define the Nevanlinna characteristic T⁡(r,ζ)T(r,\\zeta) and study its growth33endnote: 3 For a meromorphic function ff in the complex plane, the Nevanlinna characteristic is defined by T⁡(r,f)=m⁡(r,f)+N⁡(r,f),r>0,T(r,f)=m(r,f)+N(r,f),\\hskip 20.00003ptr>0, where m⁡(r,f)=12​π​∫02​πlog+⁡|f⁡(r​ei​θ)|​𝑑θm(r,f)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int\\_{0}^{2\\pi}\\log^{+}\\!\\big|f\\!\\left(re^{i\\theta}\\right)\\big|\\,d\\theta is the proximity function, and N⁡(r,f)=∫0rn⁡(t,f)−n⁡(0,f)t​𝑑t+n⁡(0,f)​log⁡rN(r,f)=\\int\\_{0}^{r}\\frac{n(t,f)-n(0,f)}{t}\\,dt+n(0,f)\\log r is the integrated counting function of poles, with n⁡(t,f)n(t,f) denoting the number of poles of ff (with multiplicity) in |z|≤t|z|\\leq t. For an entire function, N⁡(r,f)=0N(r,f)=0 and hence T⁡(r,f)=m⁡(r,f)T(r,f)=m(r,f). In the case of the Riemann zeta function ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s), which is meromorphic in ℂ\\mathbb{C} with a single pole at s=1s=1, one can define T⁡(r,ζ)T(r,\\zeta) in the same way. A classical result of Borchsenius and Jessen (1948) shows that T⁡(r,ζ)∼log⁡r2​π​∫−ππlog+⁡|ζ⁡(r​ei​θ)|​𝑑θ(r→∞),T(r,\\zeta)\\sim\\frac{\\log r}{2\\pi}\\int\\_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\log^{+}\\!\\big|\\zeta\\!\\left(re^{i\\theta}\\right)\\big|\\,d\\theta\\hskip 20.00003pt(r\\to\\infty), and, more precisely, they obtained the asymptotic T⁡(r,ζ)∼1π​r​log⁡ras ​r→∞.T(r,\\zeta)\\sim\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\,r\\log r\\hskip 10.00002pt\\text{as }r\\to\\infty. Thus the Nevanlinna characteristic of ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s) grows essentially like r​log⁡rr\\log r, which is of the same order as the growth of log⁡M⁡(r,ζ)\\log M(r,\\zeta), where M⁡(r,ζ)=max|s|=r⁡|ζ⁡(s)|M(r,\\zeta)=\\max\\_{|s|=r}|\\zeta(s)|.. The theory provides tools like the First and Second Main Theorems that relate the distribution of aa-points (solutions to f⁡(z)=af(z)=a) to the growth of the function. While direct applications to prove RH have not succeeded, Nevanlinna theory offers insights into the exceptional nature of the value 00 for ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s) as shown by the Borchsenius-Jessen theorem discussed below, and provides analogies with Picard-type theorems [[49](#bib.bib49)]. Recent work has explored connections between Nevanlinna theory and the universality properties of ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s). #### 2.2.3 Average behavior of log⁡|ζ⁡(s)|\\log|\\zeta(s)| and zeros of ζ⁡(s)−x\\zeta(s)-x The Borchsenius-Jessen theorem [[7](#bib.bib7)] provides a striking contrast to RH. While all nontrivial zeros of ζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s) are conjectured to lie on ℜ⁡(s)=1/2\\Re(s)=1/2, the zeros of ζ⁡(s)−x\\zeta(s)-x for any x≠0x\\neq 0 cluster densely near the critical line from the right. Specifically: For x≠0x\\neq 0, the equation ζ⁡(s)=x\\zeta(s)=x has infinitely many solutions in any strip 1/21/2\\Re(s)>1/2 and 01/2\\sigma>1/2, the values 1T​∫0Tf⁡(log⁡ζ⁡(σ+i​t))​𝑑t\\frac{1}{T}\\int\\_{0}^{T}f(\\log\\zeta(\\sigma+it))dt converge as T→∞T\\rightarrow\\infty for continuous bounded functions f:ℂ→ℂf:\\mathbb{C}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{C} The limiting distribution μσ\\mu\\_{\\sigma} is a probability measure on ℂ\\mathbb{C} such that: - It has no atoms (continuous distribution) - Its support is the whole complex plane when 1/20\\epsilon>0, then there exists t0t\\_{0} such that | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | max|s|≤r⁡|f⁡(s)−ζ⁡(s+34+i​t0)|0\\epsilon>0, then there exists t0t\\_{0} such that | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | maxs∈K⁡|ζ⁡(s+i​t0)−f⁡(s)|5040n>5040, where σ⁡(n)\\sigma(n) is, as above, the sum of divisors function and γ\\gamma is the Euler-Mascheroni constant. This remarkable result translates the complex-analytic RH into purely arithmetic terms. #### 4.4.2 Lagarias’ Criterion (2002) Jeffrey Lagarias refined Robin’s criterion in the form | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | R​H⇔σ⁡(n)0(Ei⁡(ρ​log​x)+Ei⁡(ρ¯​log​x))+∫x∞1t2−1​d​tt​log⁡t−log⁡2f(x)=\\operatorname{Li}(x)-\\sum\\_{\\Im(\\rho)>0}\\left(\\operatorname{Ei}(\\rho\\log x)+\\operatorname{Ei}(\\overline{\\rho}\\log x)\\right)+\\int\\_{x}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{t^{2}-1}\\frac{dt}{t\\log t}-\\log 2 (13) where the sum is over the zeros of zeta with positive imaginary part. the middle term is written as a sum of the integral logarithm Li\\operatorname{Li} function evaluated on xρx^{\\rho} where the ρ\\rho’s are the non-trivial zeros of ζ\\zeta. Now this is obviously a nonsensical sum because xx to the power ρ\\rho does not change if one replaces ρ\\rho by ρ+2​π​i​n/log⁡x\\rho+2\\pi in/\\log x, where nn is an arbitrary integer. So the constancy of Li⁡(xρ)\\operatorname{Li}(x^{\\rho}) on this arithmetic progression implies that there is no possible meaning to the infinite sum because it includes infinitely many repetitions of essentially the same term, since the complex numbers xρx^{\\rho} accumulate infinitely at least on the circle of radius x1/2x^{1/2}. In fact, von Mangoldt gave, many years after your paper, a detailed proof of your theorem, and he was more careful, up to some extent, in writing the formula … since instead of dealing with Li\\operatorname{Li} he considers a well defined univalent function Ei⁡(z)=Li⁡(exp⁡(z))\\operatorname{Ei}(z)=\\operatorname{Li}(\\exp(z)) of the correct variable zz (rather than exp⁡(z)\\exp(z)). He then proves your formula replacing the term ∑Li⁡(xρ)\\sum\\operatorname{Li}(x^{\\rho}) by ∑Ei⁡(ρ​log⁡x)\\sum\\operatorname{Ei}(\\rho\\log x). This being said, it is clear from your formula that you knew what is now called the Guinand-Weil explicit formulas. And the new input in this formula, but that wouldn’t surprise you, is that one considers, instead of the function 1/log⁡u1/\\log u which you use, an arbitrary test function ϕ⁡(u)\\phi(u) applied on the prime powers, and one writes an equality which involves what is nowadays called the Mellin transform99 9 The Mellin transform of ϕ⁡(u)\\phi(u) is defined as ℳ⁡(ϕ)​(s)=∫0∞us−1​ϕ​(u)​𝑑u\\mathcal{M}(\\phi)(s)=\\int\\_{0}^{\\infty}u^{s-1}\\phi(u)du of this function ϕ⁡(u)\\phi(u) evaluated on the non-trivial zeros of the zeta function. One obtains in this way an equality which is not really a generalization of your formula in the sense that yours goes one level of subtlety beyond these classical Guinand-Weil explicit formulas since your test function 1/log⁡u1/\\log u is singular at u=1u=1. One thing which is striking in these explicit formulas is that if the test function ϕ⁡(u)\\phi(u) vanishes outside a finite interval [1,x][1,x], then only finitely many primes are involved in its computation, those less than xx. But this is of course the case for your formula which involves the function equal to ϕ⁡(u)=1/log⁡u\\phi(u)=1/\\log u in the interval [1,x][1,x] and vanishing outside, so this would not be a surprise to you. But let me tell you, Master, a fact which I find quite supportive of your hypothesis. The fact is the following. I will devise a process with which, by using only a few primes, I will be able to reach the first non-trivial zeros of your zeta function with remarkable precision. Now what do I mean by involving a few primes? I will take a concrete situation and I will imagine that I do not know what are the primes beyond 13. So I only know 2, 3, 5, 7, 11 and 13. Now at first it seems that in order to get any grasp on the zeros of zeta, it is necessary to involve all primes, because all of them are involved in the Euler product which is defining this function. And so it seems quite unbelievable that by truncating this Euler product, namely by only considering the terms in the Euler product which are involving the primes smaller than 13, one could get any hint on the zeros of the zeta function. So let me describe the process using only notions that are familiar to you. Out of these primes, 2, 3, etc., up to 13, one fabricates a quadratic form. This quadratic form is similar to the quadratic form you were using when you applied the Dirichlet principle in proving the conformal mapping theorem. It is a quadratic form on the infinite dimensional space of functions ϕ⁡(u)\\phi(u) of a positive real variable which vanish outside the interval [1,13][1,13]. The value Q⁡(ϕ)Q(\\phi) of the quadratic form is obtained by applying the explicit formula to the function ψ⁡(v)=∫ϕ⁡(u)​ϕ​(u​v)​𝑑u/u\\psi(v)=\\int\\phi(u)\\phi(uv)du/u. Thus because the function ϕ\\phi vanish outside the interval [1,13][1,13], the function ψ\\psi vanishes outside the interval [1/13,13][1/13,13] and one does not need to use any other prime power than 2,3,4,5,7,8,9,11,132,3,4,5,7,8,9,11,13 to compute Q⁡(ϕ)Q(\\phi). Next, I know how to prove that there is a function η⁡(u)\\eta(u) realizing the minimum of the quadratic form Q⁡(ϕ)Q(\\phi) while ∫ϕ​(u)2​𝑑u/u=1\\int\\phi(u)^{2}du/u=1. The proof of existence is entirely similar to the proof given by Hilbert in 1900, in his paper \"Über das Dirichletsche Prinzip\" for the Dirichlet principle. I then take the Mellin transform of the function η⁡(u)\\eta(u). I also know how to prove1010 10 See §[6.1](#S6.SS1 \"6.1 The Fourier transform of θ x has all its zeros on the real line ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\") that the zeros of this Mellin transform are on the critical line1111 11 Which is normalized here as the imaginary line. This is proved modulo a condition of uniqueness of the minimum1212 12 The proof uses a generalization of a Theorem of Caratheodory-Fejer on Toeplitz matrices, obtained in 1911, one needs to assume that the lowest eigenvalue of the quadratic form is simple and even. The amazing fact, which I want to report to you, is that when one computes—with the aid of modern computational machines far beyond what was available in your time—the zeros of the Mellin transform of η⁡(u)\\eta(u), say its first 50 zeros, one finds an incredible coincidence with the non-trivial zeros of zeta. For instance, for the first zero, you find out that there are 54 decimal places which agree, and so on and so forth. The number of decimal places which agree decreases slowly, and when you reach the 50-th zero, only few decimal places agree. I have computed these differences (upper bound of) between the values computed using primes less than 13 and the true values of zeros of zeta which I present herewith: Differences Between Values (using primes ≤13\\leq 13) | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | | 2.60179×10−55, 4.80071×10−52, 4.43756×10−50, 3.89903×10−47, 7.59453×10−46, 1.13198×10−43,\\displaystyle 2.60179\\times 10^{-55},\\,4.80071\\times 10^{-52},\\,4.43756\\times 10^{-50},\\,3.89903\\times 10^{-47},\\,7.59453\\times 10^{-46},\\,1.13198\\times 10^{-43}, | | | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | | 1.07245×10−41, 1.2694×10−40, 4.40141×10−38, 4.24869×10−37, 5.86724×10−36, 3.24443×10−34,\\displaystyle 1.07245\\times 10^{-41},\\,1.2694\\times 10^{-40},\\,4.40141\\times 10^{-38},\\,4.24869\\times 10^{-37},\\,5.86724\\times 10^{-36},\\,3.24443\\times 10^{-34}, | | | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | | 2.44517×10−32, 9.02026×10−32, 5.13539×10−30, 7.04142×10−29, 6.47754×10−28, 4.96772×10−27,\\displaystyle 2.44517\\times 10^{-32},\\,9.02026\\times 10^{-32},\\,5.13539\\times 10^{-30},\\,7.04142\\times 10^{-29},\\,6.47754\\times 10^{-28},\\,4.96772\\times 10^{-27}, | | | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | | 5.86016×10−25, 3.76751×10−24, 1.03779×10−23, 3.52722×10−22, 3.03977×10−21, 5.66201×10−20,\\displaystyle 5.86016\\times 10^{-25},\\,3.76751\\times 10^{-24},\\,1.03779\\times 10^{-23},\\,3.52722\\times 10^{-22},\\,3.03977\\times 10^{-21},\\,5.66201\\times 10^{-20}, | | | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | | 1.41755×10−19, 2.19821×10−18, 6.31599×10−17, 1.42037×10−16, 4.34328×10−16, 4.47113×10−15,\\displaystyle 1.41755\\times 10^{-19},\\,2.19821\\times 10^{-18},\\,6.31599\\times 10^{-17},\\,1.42037\\times 10^{-16},\\,4.34328\\times 10^{-16},\\,4.47113\\times 10^{-15}, | | | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | | 7.01522×10−14, 3.81989×10−13, 5.99581×10−13, 4.26414×10−11, 1.10653×10−10, 1.95651×10−10,\\displaystyle 7.01522\\times 10^{-14},\\,3.81989\\times 10^{-13},\\,5.99581\\times 10^{-13},\\,4.26414\\times 10^{-11},\\,1.10653\\times 10^{-10},\\,1.95651\\times 10^{-10}, | | | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | | 5.20728×10−10, 2.05031×10−9, 3.42274×10−8, 2.10931×10−7, 2.23714×10−7, 5.95608×10−7,\\displaystyle 5.20728\\times 10^{-10},\\,2.05031\\times 10^{-9},\\,3.42274\\times 10^{-8},\\,2.10931\\times 10^{-7},\\,2.23714\\times 10^{-7},\\,5.95608\\times 10^{-7}, | | | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | | 5.77737×10−6, 0.000141389, 0.000556111, 0.000720794, 0.000314865, 0.0209081, 0.00313565, 0.00212727\\displaystyle 5.77737\\times 10^{-6},\\,0.000141389,\\,0.000556111,\\,0.000720794,\\,0.000314865,\\,0.0209081,\\,0.00313565,\\,0.00212727 | | What this is saying is that we have a firm grasp on your zeros, without at any point involving the infinity of the collection of all primes. And moreover we know a priori that all zeros of the Mellin transform of η⁡(u)\\eta(u) are on the critical line. What we do not know is that, when we increase the upper limit, which was x=13x=13 here, the corresponding set of zeros will converge towards the zeros of zeta. This is something which at this point is not proved. On the other hand, it seems that the abstract reason why your conjecture is true is now within reach since we know that the zeros of the Mellin transforms of the minimal eigenvectors ηx\\eta\\_{x} are purely imaginary, and we expect that the ηx\\eta\\_{x} converge to the function whose Mellin transform is your function Ξ⁡(i​t)\\Xi(it). The result would then follow from Hurwitz’s theorem, which implies that all the zeros of the limit of a convergent sequence1313 13 uniformly on compact subsets of holomorphic functions whose zeros are on a fixed line are still on that line. With respect and admiration, Alain Connes ## 6 The strategy and the next small steps We first explain in §[6.1](#S6.SS1 \"6.1 The Fourier transform of θ x has all its zeros on the real line ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\") the general result which provides a wealth of entire functions all of whose zeros are on the real line. The goal then is to try to show that, when the upper bound xx (which was x=13x=13 above) tends to infinity, the minimal eigenvectors ηx\\eta\\_{x} converge in a suitable sense so that their Fourier transforms, for the duality between ℝ+∗{\\mathbb{R}}^{\\*}\\_{+} and ℝ{\\mathbb{R}}, will converge uniformly on compact subsets to the holomorphic function that Riemann had introduced as the Ξ\\Xi-function in his paper. In fact we shall follow his footsteps and consider the explicit formula he gave for the function kk whose Fourier transform is Ξ⁡(t)\\Xi(t). We shall rewrite in §[6.2](#S6.SS2 \"6.2 Riemann’s Ξ function and Hermite functions ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\") this function kk as the image by a summation map ℰ{\\mathcal{E}} of a linear combination hh of two Hermite functions h0,h4h\\_{0},h\\_{4}. The issue, then, is to show the convergence of the minimal eigenvectors ηx\\eta\\_{x} to the function ℰ⁡(h){\\mathcal{E}}(h). In fact, one first needs to recenter ηx\\eta\\_{x} to θx​(u)=ηx​(x1/2​u)\\theta\\_{x}(u)=\\eta\\_{x}(x^{1/2}u) which has its support in [x−1/2,x1/2][x^{-1/2},x^{1/2}]. The plan, in order to achieve this goal, is to break it into several small steps. In the second step, §[6.3](#S6.SS3 \"6.3 The prolate wave functions enter the scene ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\"), one introduces, given λ>1\\lambda>1, the prolate spheroidal wave functions which give an adaptation hn,λh\\_{n,\\lambda} of the hermite functions hnh\\_{n} to functions with support in the interval [−λ,λ]⊂ℝ[-\\lambda,\\lambda]\\subset{\\mathbb{R}}. The functions hn,λh\\_{n,\\lambda} are eigenfunctions of a modification of the Hermite operator ([16](#S6.E16 \"In 6.2 Riemann’s Ξ function and Hermite functions ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\")), the prolate wave operator PWλ\\operatorname{PW}\\_{\\lambda} of ([18](#S6.E18 \"In 6.3 The prolate wave functions enter the scene ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\")), obtained by adding to the Hermite operator 𝐇\\mathbf{H} the only multiple of the square of the scaling so that the sum commutes with the orthogonal projection on functions with support in the interval [−λ,λ]⊂ℝ[-\\lambda,\\lambda]\\subset{\\mathbb{R}}. By construction PWλ\\operatorname{PW}\\_{\\lambda} admits two regular singular points at the boundary ±λ\\pm\\lambda of the interval and an irregular singularity at ∞\\infty. The third step §[6.4](#S6.SS4 \"6.4 The Poisson formula and the approximation k λ of θ x , = λ x . ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\"), consists in making an educated guess for an approximation of the minimal eigenvector θx\\theta\\_{x} by using the same process as in the simple case of §[6.2](#S6.SS2 \"6.2 Riemann’s Ξ function and Hermite functions ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\"). Thus one simply replaces the Hermite functions h0,h4h\\_{0},h\\_{4} by their localizations h0,λ,h4,λh\\_{0,\\lambda},h\\_{4,\\lambda}, for λ=x1/2\\lambda=x^{1/2}, and one applies the summation map ℰ{\\mathcal{E}} to the linear combination of these two functions with vanishing integral. One obtains in this manner a function kλk\\_{\\lambda} which approximates θx\\theta\\_{x} on the interval [x−1/2,x1/2][x^{-1/2},x^{1/2}]. In §[6.5](#S6.SS5 \"6.5 Convergence of the Fourier tranforms → ^ k λ ^ k ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\") one shows that, when λ→∞\\lambda\\to\\infty, the Fourier transforms k^λ\\widehat{k}\\_{\\lambda} converge towards the Ξ\\Xi function of Riemann, uniformly on closed substrips of the open strip of width 11 neighborhood of the real line. Finally in §[6.6](#S6.SS6 \"6.6 Remaining steps ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\") we point to the missing final steps. ### 6.1 The Fourier transform of θx\\theta\\_{x} has all its zeros on the real line The proof of this result follows from a theorem shown in a joint paper [[32](#bib.bib32)] with Walter van Suijlekom, entitled \"Quadratic Forms, Real Zeros and Echoes of the Spectral Action\". The precise statement is : ###### Theorem 6.1. Let L>0L>0, 𝒟\\mathcal{D} be a real distribution on the interval [0,L][0,L] and 𝒟~\\tilde{\\mathcal{D}} the associated even distribution on [−L,L][-L,L]. Assume that the quadratic form with Schwartz kernel 𝒟~​(x−y)\\tilde{\\mathcal{D}}(x-y) defines a lower-bounded selfadjoint operator on L2​([−L2,L2])L^{2}([-\\frac{L}{2},\\frac{L}{2}]), and that the minimum of its spectrum is a simple, isolated eigenvalue, with even eigenfunction η\\eta. Then all the zeros of the entire function η^​(z)\\widehat{\\eta}(z), z∈ℂz\\in\\mathbb{C}, Fourier transform of η\\eta lie on the real line. The proof of this theorem is based on the special form of the matrix of the quadratic form in the trigonometric orthonormal basis, the construction for finite matrices of that special form of a selfadjoint operator and the above mentioned Hurwitz theorem to pass to the limit when the size of the matrices tends to infinity. The validity of the result on the zeros of the Fourier transform for finite truncations [[32](#bib.bib32)] plays a key role in numerical computations1414 14 It is used for the trigonometric truncation at N=100N=100 in the computation presented in the letter and allows one to approximate the zeros of η^​(z)\\widehat{\\eta}(z) by the spectrum of a rank one perturbation of the periodic Dirac operator, obtained using the Dirichlet kernel analysed by Riemann in [[91](#bib.bib91)]. ### 6.2 Riemann’s Ξ\\Xi function and Hermite functions Riemann adopts the following notation | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | ψ⁡(x):=∑1∞e−n2​π​x\\psi(x):=\\sum\\_{1}^{\\infty}e^{-n^{2}\\pi x} | | and then writes, with ξ(s):=12s(s−1)π−s/2ζ(s)Γ(s2)\\xi(s):=\\frac{1}{2}s(s-1)\\pi^{-s/2}\\zeta(s)\\Gamma\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right), Ξ⁡(t):=ξ⁡(s)\\Xi(t):=\\xi(s) for s=12+i​ts=\\frac{1}{2}+it, the equality1515 15 The traditional notation for this function is Ξ⁡(t)\\Xi(t) instead of Riemann’s ξ⁡(t)\\xi(t). | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | Ξ⁡(t)=4​∫1∞d⁡(x32​ψ′​(x))d​x​x−14​cos​(12​t​log⁡x)​𝑑x\\Xi(t)=4\\int\\_{1}^{\\infty}\\frac{d\\left(x^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\psi^{\\prime}(x)\\right)}{dx}x^{-\\frac{1}{4}}{{\\rm cos}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}t\\log x\\right)dx | | He then obtains, using the equality k⁡(u)=k⁡(u−1)k(u)=k(u^{-1}) from the Poisson formula, where | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | k⁡(u):=u1/2​π2​∑1∞n2​u2​(2​π​n2​u2−3)​e−π​n2​u2k(u):=u^{1/2}\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sum\\_{1}^{\\infty}n^{2}u^{2}\\left(2\\pi n^{2}u^{2}-3\\right)e^{-\\pi n^{2}u^{2}} | | that Ξ⁡(t)\\Xi(t) is the Fourier transform of the function k⁡(u)k(u) : | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | Ξ⁡(t)=2​∫1∞k⁡(u)​cos​(t​log⁡u)​d∗​u=∫0∞k⁡(u)​ui​t​d∗​u,\\Xi(t)=2\\int\\_{1}^{\\infty}k(u){{\\rm cos}}\\left(t\\log u\\right)d^{\\*}u=\\int\\_{0}^{\\infty}k(u)u^{it}d^{\\*}u,\\ \\ | | Moreover introducing the notation | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | ℰ⁡(f)​(u):=u1/2​∑1∞f⁡(n​u){\\mathcal{E}}(f)(u):=u^{1/2}\\sum\\_{1}^{\\infty}f(nu) | | (14) | one has | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | k⁡(u)=ℰ⁡(h)​(u),h⁡(u)=π2​u2​(2​π​u2−3)​e−π​u2.k(u)={\\mathcal{E}}(h)(u),\\ \\ h(u)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}u^{2}\\left(2\\pi u^{2}-3\\right)e^{-\\pi u^{2}}. | | (15) | The function h⁡(u)h(u) can be characterized as follows. One considers the Hermite operator (harmonic oscillator) | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | 𝐇​f​(u):=−f′′​(u)+4​π2​u2​f​(u)\\mathbf{H}f(u):=-f^{\\prime\\prime}(u)+4\\pi^{2}u^{2}f(u) | | (16) | and lets hnh\\_{n} be the normalized eigenfunction for the eigenvalue 2​π​(1+2​n)2\\pi(1+2n). These functions are even for nn even and invariant under the Fourier transform for nn multiple of 44. ###### Fact 6.2. The Ξ\\Xi function of Riemann is the Fourier transform of k=ℰ⁡(h)k={\\mathcal{E}}(h) where hh is, up to a multiplicative scalar, the only linear combination of h0,h4h\\_{0},h\\_{4} with vanishing integral88endnote: 8 More precisely one has h=34 23/4​h4−3217/4​h0,‖hj‖=1,‖h‖=33217/4.h=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4\\ 2^{3/4}}h\\_{4}-\\frac{3}{2^{17/4}}h\\_{0},\\ \\ \\|h\\_{j}\\|=1,\\ \\ \\|h\\|=\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{2^{17/4}}. (17) . ### 6.3 The prolate wave functions enter the scene The next step requires introducing a family of functions whose pivotal role in signal transmission was uncovered by Slepian, Pollak, and Landau at Bell Labs during the 1960s. Their work addressed a fundamental question first posed by Claude Shannon in his foundational study on entropy1616 16 C. E. Shannon, *Communication in the Presence of Noise*, Proc. IRE 37 (1949), 10–21.: *To what extent can functions that are band-limited also be time-limited?* Shannon had estimated that the number NN of orthogonal signals that can be packed into a time window TT and frequency band WW satisfies N≃2​T​WN\\simeq 2TW. Building on this, Slepian and his collaborators sought to maximize the information transmittable over a limited time within a given bandwidth — a problem central to efficient communication systems. To describe their result, fix the time and frequency interval as [−λ,λ]⊂ℝ[-\\lambda,\\lambda]\\subset\\mathbb{R}, and let PλP\\_{\\lambda} be the projection in the Hilbert space L2​(ℝ)L^{2}(\\mathbb{R}) (square-integrable functions) defined by multiplication with the characteristic function of the interval [−λ,λ][-\\lambda,\\lambda]. Let Pλ^:=𝔽eℝ​Pλ​𝔽eℝ−1\\widehat{P\\_{\\lambda}}:=\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}P\\_{\\lambda}\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}^{-1} be its conjugate under the Fourier transform,1717 17 The normalized Fourier transform is defined by 𝔽eℝ​(f)​(y):=∫ℝf⁡(x)​e2​π​i​x​y​𝑑x.\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}(f)(y):=\\int\\_{\\mathbb{R}}f(x)e^{2\\pi ixy}\\,dx. Note that on even functions one has 𝔽eℝ−1=𝔽eℝ\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}^{-1}=\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}. In their seminal paper, [[101](#bib.bib101)], Slepian and Pollak showed that the compressed Fourier transform Pλ​𝔽eℝ​PλP\\_{\\lambda}\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}P\\_{\\lambda} commutes with the second-order differential operator | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | PWλ:=−∂x((λ2−x2)∂x)+(2πλx)2.\\operatorname{PW}\\_{\\lambda}:=-\\partial\\_{x}\\left((\\lambda^{2}-x^{2})\\partial\\_{x}\\right)+(2\\pi\\lambda x)^{2}. | | (18) | It follows that this operator commutes on L2​(ℝ)evL^{2}({\\mathbb{R}})^{\\mathrm{ev}} (even functions) with | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | Pλ​Pλ^​Pλ=Pλ​𝔽eℝ​Pλ​𝔽eℝ​Pλ=(Pλ​𝔽eℝ​Pλ)2,P\\_{\\lambda}\\widehat{P\\_{\\lambda}}P\\_{\\lambda}=P\\_{\\lambda}\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}P\\_{\\lambda}\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}P\\_{\\lambda}=\\left(P\\_{\\lambda}\\mathbb{F}\\_{e\\_{\\mathbb{R}}}P\\_{\\lambda}\\right)^{2}, | | which enables one to diagonalize the *angle operator*99endnote: 9 The notion of angle between two closed subspaces of a Hilbert space — or equivalently, between two orthogonal projections PP and QQ — was studied by J. Dixmier in: *Position relative de deux variétés linéaires fermées dans un espace de Hilbert*, Revue Sci. 86 (1948), 387–399. The key idea is that a pair (P,Q)(P,Q) corresponds to a unitary representation of the infinite dihedral group. Its irreducible representations are classified by an angle α\\alpha, which in the real plane represents the angle between two real lines. The relation P​cos2​α=P​Q​PP{{\\rm cos}}^{2}\\alpha=PQP determines the angle operator on each irreducible component. αλ\\alpha\\_{\\lambda} between the projections PλP\\_{\\lambda} and Pλ^\\widehat{P\\_{\\lambda}} defined by the identity | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | Pλ​cos2​(αλ)=Pλ​Pλ^​Pλ.P\\_{\\lambda}{{\\rm cos}}^{2}(\\alpha\\_{\\lambda})=P\\_{\\lambda}\\widehat{P\\_{\\lambda}}P\\_{\\lambda}. | | ###### Fact 6.3. The eigenvalues of the operator Pλ​Pλ^​PλP\\_{\\lambda}\\widehat{P\\_{\\lambda}}P\\_{\\lambda} in L2​([−λ,λ])evL^{2}([-\\lambda,\\lambda])^{\\mathrm{ev}} are simple and form a decreasing sequence νn​(λ)\\nu\\_{n}(\\lambda), n≥0n\\geq 0, νn​(λ)→0\\nu\\_{n}(\\lambda)\\to 0 for n→∞n\\to\\infty, such that 1>ν0​(λ)>ν1​(λ>…>0𝐶𝐿𝑂𝑆𝐸1>\\nu\\_{0}(\\lambda)>\\nu\\_{1}(\\lambda>\\ldots>0. The corresponding eigenfunctions are the *prolate spheroidal wave functions* of even index h2​n,λh\\_{2n,\\lambda} where hm,λh\\_{m,\\lambda} is the m+1m+1-th eigenfunction of the prolate wave operator ([18](#S6.E18 \"In 6.3 The prolate wave functions enter the scene ‣ 6 The strategy and the next small steps ‣ The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\")) in L2​([−λ,λ])L^{2}([-\\lambda,\\lambda]). The operator αλ\\alpha\\_{\\lambda} has a finite number1818 18 With our normalization, time duration is T=2​λT=2\\lambda and bandlimit is W=λW=\\lambda. ∼4​λ2\\sim 4\\lambda^{2} of small nonzero eigenvalues. Following the standard notation in the literature, the prolate spheroidal wave functions are denoted | | | | | --- | --- | --- | | | hn,λ​(x):=PSn,0​(2​π​λ2,xλ)h\\_{n,\\lambda}(x):=\\mathrm{PS}\\_{n,0}\\left(2\\pi\\lambda^{2},\\frac{x}{